EUR/USD traded 1.3593-1.3667, USD/JPY a no-show between 81.99-82.21 while AUD/USD slipped to 1.0114 from 1.0192. Elsewhere, the Bank of Greece \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rapid fall in the Egyptian pound seen since domestic banks reopened Monday. USD/EGP collapsed to 5.8751 after printing a fresh 10-year high of 5.9651 Tuesday. EUR/SEK printed yet another 10-year low of 8.7643. Away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets core European government bonds trade flat although lower demand at the ECB's weekly and monthly cash tenders pushed short-term German yields higher. March bunds are currently -0.01 at 122.85, March gilts +0.02 at 115.85. The main European equity bourses trade 0.1% around par after taking a small dip on China's rate hike. Oil's $1.50 drop to a one-week low of $85.95 is all related to China while gold is a down 60c at $1350oz. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1305 GMT [Dow Jones] Traders shrugged off a second straight disappointing report on Germany's economy and an interest-rate hike by China to send the euro higher against the dollar. Some risk appetite returned to the market as market participants downplayed the large-than-expected drop in Germany industrial production in December. China hiked benchmark rates by 0.25 percentage points. The euro was at $1.3632 from $1.3582 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1304 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  still expects another two additional rate hikes in China in the first-half of the year, based on the view that inflation will peak in 1Q 2011. "We expect consumer price inflation to again surge above 5% year-on-year in January from 4.4% year-on-year," the bank adds. Danske says Tuesday's rate hike suggests that China might hike more this year than it is currently expected. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1249 GMT (Dow Jones) Egyptian bonds continue their recovery Tuesday as tensions continue to ease. Egypt's 3.75% 2020 bond is currently trading at 96.70, up from 92.19 at the height of the crisis. Meanwhile, Egypt's 6.875% bonds maturing April 30, 2040 are currently trading at 93.95 compared with a low Jan 31 of 88.50. The bond market recovery is broadly in line with the country's five-year CDS spread which is now trading at 343bps, around 90bps tighter than the late January high of 432bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate hike Tuesday should be supportive of assets in "Goldilocks Asia" countries such as Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and Malaysia, as it removes uncertainty over Chinese policy, says Prasenjit K. Basu, chief Asia ex-Japan economist at Daiwa Capital Markets. "With export prospects bolstered by the surging U.S. economy, the PBOC has again demonstrated its determination to rein in CPI inflation and stabilize the property market. We believe China's CPI inflation is close to peaking, and this hike will make a salutary contribution toward ensuring that," he says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads are 12 bps higher to 785 bps as the market shrugs off a small but positive T-Bill auction which was largely expected and factored-in. Turnover on the local HDAT and European trading platform adds only to EUR15M. "News that a small Danish bank is insolvent and will be nationalized and unwound spooked the GGB market because the senior bond holders will take losses," says a bond analyst. "All the euro zone periphery is moving wider and the market mood suggests that the trend will continue as profit taking sets in and risk appetite eases." (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1228 GMT (Dow Jones)--Troika Dialog recommends going long the ruble against the dollar euro basket to benefit from positive Russia sentiment and higher oil prices. "If oil prices stay at this level, or at least do not fall below $80/bbl, we should see a much stronger ruble. The tax payment period, which starts next week, will provide further support to the ruble. Our next target is RUB33.50 versus the basket." Ruble now trading at RUB34.09 against the basket, weaker by 0.3%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1228 GMT [Dow Jones] Interest rate hikes in China will do little to stem upward pressure on food and energy prices and have limited impact on growing signs of spillover price effects into non-food consumer inflation, says Monument Securities analyst Marc Ostwald. PBOC's benchmark interest rate hike Tuesday and upward pressure on official rates across Asia due to high inflation will fuel concerns about growth outlook, but momentum underlying growth in developing economies is stronger than many realize and is increasingly internal, he says. (owen.fletcher@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1206 GMT (Dow Jones) As well as further China rate hikes, CNY appreciation will likely also be part of Beijing's efforts to curb inflation over 2011, with officials signalling a greater willingness to tolerate CNY strength, says RBC Emerging Markets. The bank has a year-end USD/CNY forecast of 6.20 against that implied by the NDF market of 6.45. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1159 GMT (Dow Jones) Recent strong economic data prompts \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  to lift its forecast for 10-year U.S. Treasury yields to 3.5% for the end of 1Q, versus a previous forecast of 3.25%. The bank sticks to its end-2011 and end-2012 projections of 3.8% and 4.3%, respectively. 10-year Treasurys yield 3.635% Tuesday. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1147 GMT (Dow Jones) Hungary's weaker than expected December industrial output will likely be an additional argument to stop the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from raising rates in February, Citi economist Piotr Kalisz says. December output dropped by 11.8% on month and was only up 6% on year. Citi says the weak data is likely to be due to a one-off item, "a result of some statistical aberration," saying actual trend changes are typically much more gradual. Expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to leave rates on hold at 6%, waiting for details of the government's fiscal austerity package, to be announced by end-February. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com) [ 02-08-11 0820ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110208e7280008d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
