\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China's JGB Sales Don't Presage Selloff-Economist\b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 02:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The strong yen may have been a factor in China's net sale of Y81.3 billion of Japan financial assets in November, most of which are thought to be JGBs, as the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have prompted Beijing to take profits on its government bond holdings, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute, who adds, no sharp selloff is expected ahead. China bought a net Y262.5 billion of Japanese financial assets in October, according to current account data from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kusaba says as long as China continues to keep the CNY on a leash, which requires it to regularly buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the Chinese unit from rising, it will likely continue trending toward net JPY asset purchases.\par \par \uc2  "There has been no change to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so even though we may see some Chinese JGB selling to lock in profits when the yen rises, that doesn't mean China will be dumping Japanese bonds ahead." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-01-11 0153GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1012 1037 1085 1161 5011 5063 55113 55117 75112   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110112e71c00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
