\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Indonesia Shares Likely Up; 3550 Resistance Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 03:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are likely higher, helped by gains in most Asian markets, which in turn rise on U.S. stocks' higher finish overnight as debt concerns in Europe eased, traders say. They expect the main index to top 3550 after it ended down 0.7% Wednesday at 3455.127. The main index has fallen 6.7% since the start of 2011. Foreigners have been net sellers of IDR4.74 trillion worth of shares in the previous 4 sessions. "We expect that foreign funds may gradually return to buy Indonesian shares amid hopes for strong corporate fourth-quarter earnings," says a fund manager at a state-owned pension fund. Commodity-related stocks such as coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) and palm oil producer Astra Agro (AALI.JK) may lead gains on higher commodity prices overnight. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  is expected to raise its policy rate for a second-straight month (decision due 0730 GMT) amid increasing inflationary pressures; 10 of 13 economists surveyed by Dow Jones forecast a 25bp increase to 2.25%, while 3 expect no change. BOT Governor Prasarn Trairatvorakul last month said the interest rate cycle remains on an uptrend and added that leaving real interest rates negative for too long could hurt the economy. Expectations of another increase swelled after December inflation rose 3.0% on-year with core inflation--which strips out energy and food costs--up 1.4% on-year (still within the BOT's target range of 0.5%-3.0%). "On average during 1999-2009, the policy rate was around one percentage point higher than the core CPI. Given the BOT's forecast of core CPI at 2%-3% for 2011, it implies that the BOT may like the policy rate to peak at 3%-4% in this rate-hike cycle," says Capital Nomura Securities' economist Nuchjarin Panarode. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are steady with low volume, as most investors are on the sidelines before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's auction of CNY30 billion worth of one-year bills later this morning, the first government debt offering this year. "The bill sale may receive strong demand from investors due to the existing flush liquidity in the money market," says a Shenzhen-based trader with a local broker. The bill sale yield is likely in the range of 2.82%-2.86%, compared with the 2.91% yield on one-year bills traded in the secondary market, he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 126.42, mostly unchanged from the last close of 126.41, and is expected to hover around the current level today. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is down sharply after the central parity was set at a record low level ahead of President Hu Jintao's U.S. visit. The pair is at 6.6124 vs 6.6200 late Tuesday, after the parity is set at 6.6128 vs 6.6216 Tuesday. "We are closely watching whether the parity will fall below the 6.6000 key level in the coming days," says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. He adds the USD/CNY may consolidate around the current level for the rest of the session. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD drifts lower to 29.097, compared to the 29.19-29.22 band most of yesterday. A trader from a foreign bank notes strong flows to the stock market; the Taiex is last +0.6%, extending yesterday's 1.3% gain. He tips immediate support for the pair at 29.050, and expects it's unlikely to break below the key 29.00 support ahead of the Lunar New Year holidays which start Feb. 2. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher, led by coal miners and metal firms due to rising overseas oil and metal prices on supply concerns and a weakened U.S. dollar. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.4% at 2814.62, with analysts tipping immediate resistance at 2850 today. "In the absence of fresh negative news, cheaply-valued blue chips would be attractive and will curb any downside of the market in the short term," Huatai Securities says in a note, adding some financial stocks and property developers, such as \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ), may outperform as the earning season approaches. Among advancers, coal miner Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 3.5% at CNY28.91 and China Coal (601898.SH) rises 1.5% to CNY11.05. Metal firm Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) gains 1.4% to CNY43.25 and Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) advances 0.7% to CNY48.80. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.5% at 1257.56. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares should bounce back today after 3 days of declines, which has included heavy foreign net selling; mild gains on Wall Street overnight along with a slightly more positive tone on regional markets will give sentiment a bit of a boost. KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul says investors should keep an eye on the Portugal bond sale tonight to get a better indication of European sentiment in the coming days; he adds that the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  should lift its policy rate by 25 bps to 2.25% today, which will be positive for equity inflows. BOT's policy rate decision and statement is due at 0730 GMT. Resistance is at 1025, with support at 1000. The index ends down 0.5% at 1013.39. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's smaller-than-expected current account surplus for November shows that the country's overseas shipments lost more steam near the end of last year, dragging on the export-driven economy, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute. "Exports to Asia and the U.S. lost momentum, and though the strong yen on a whole isn't uniformly bad it did weigh on exports and eat into overseas income brought back to Japan," he says. But in 2011, Kusaba says there are signs that demand for Japanese goods from China, the U.S. and other key markets is likely to increase as those economies strengthen, which could prevent sharper declines in exports, and limit the overall economic impact ahead. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The strong yen may have been a factor in China's net sale of Y81.3 billion of Japan financial assets in November, most of which are thought to be JGBs, as the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have prompted Beijing to take profits on its government bond holdings, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute, who adds, no sharp selloff is expected ahead. China bought a net Y262.5 billion of Japanese financial assets in October, according to current account data from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kusaba says as long as China continues to keep the CNY on a leash, which requires it to regularly buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the Chinese unit from rising, it will likely continue trending toward net JPY asset purchases. "There has been no change to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so even though we may see some Chinese JGB selling to lock in profits when the yen rises, that doesn't mean China will be dumping Japanese bonds ahead." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] January HSI futures are up 0.2% at 23,830, suggesting the cash market will open modestly higher. The contract ended Tuesday at a 17-point premium to cash, continuing to signal a positive near-term bias, amid the Hong Kong market's 8th day higher in the past 10 sessions. January open interest increases by 3,577 lots to 98,961. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI rises 0.2% to 3246.36 with 22 of the 30 index components trading higher after Wall Street's gain Tuesday, and amid broadly higher regional markets; there are over 3 gainers per decliner with 361 million shares traded on the broad market. From a regional perspective, Daiwa says it expects ASEAN markets to build on their gains for 2010 in 2011, "as corporate earnings ride on robust household spending and rising infrastructure investment." Though it says real-GDP and EPS growth may slow this year, "we see early signs that economic growth is broadening, which we believe bodes well for the medium-term." The house remains Neutral on Singapore in the region as "relative valuations look less compelling than for ASEAN on average." Analysts tip STI's next resistance at 3250, support at 3220. \uc2 Straits Asia Resources\uc2  (AJ1.SG) surges 7.2% to S$2.83 as investors react positively to news it was issued the Principle Licence for the Northern Leases coal mining area; Hyflux (600.SG) gains 2.1% to S$2.39 on project wins in China. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends its fall and is testing the 1,120 support amid selling by offshore players; the pair is now at 1,120.40 compared to 1,125.10 late yesterday in Seoul. A rebound in the EUR/USD and foreign buying in local stocks are all helping lift the local unit, says a local bank trader. He expects the USD/KRW to hover around 1,120 for now; he adds "there doesn't appear to be any (BOK) smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, but the chances are for them to kick in later" in support of USD. The trader pegs next support at 1,118. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 01-11-11 