ancers, coal miner Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 3.5% at CNY28.91 and China Coal (601898.SH) rises 1.5% to CNY11.05. Metal firm Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) gains 1.4% to CNY43.25 and Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) advances 0.7% to CNY48.80. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.5% at 1257.56. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares should bounce back today after 3 days of declines, which has included heavy foreign net selling; mild gains on Wall Street overnight along with a slightly more positive tone on regional markets will give sentiment a bit of a boost. KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul says investors should keep an eye on the Portugal bond sale tonight to get a better indication of European sentiment in the coming days; he adds that the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  should lift its policy rate by 25 bps to 2.25% today, which will be positive for equity inflows. BOT's policy rate decision and statement is due at 0730 GMT. Resistance is at 1025, with support at 1000. The index ends down 0.5% at 1013.39. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's smaller-than-expected current account surplus for November shows that the country's overseas shipments lost more steam near the end of last year, dragging on the export-driven economy, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute. "Exports to Asia and the U.S. lost momentum, and though the strong yen on a whole isn't uniformly bad it did weigh on exports and eat into overseas income brought back to Japan," he says. But in 2011, Kusaba says there are signs that demand for Japanese goods from China, the U.S. and other key markets is likely to increase as those economies strengthen, which could prevent sharper declines in exports, and limit the overall economic impact ahead. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The strong yen may have been a factor in China's net sale of Y81.3 billion of Japan financial assets in November, most of which are thought to be JGBs, as the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have prompted Beijing to take profits on its government bond holdings, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute, who adds, no sharp selloff is expected ahead. China bought a net Y262.5 billion of Japanese financial assets in October, according to current account data from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kusaba says as long as China continues to keep the CNY on a leash, which requires it to regularly buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the Chinese unit from rising, it will likely continue trending toward net JPY asset purchases. "There has been no change to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so even though we may see some Chinese JGB selling to lock in profits when the yen rises, that doesn't mean China will be dumping Japanese bonds ahead." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] January HSI futures are up 0.2% at 23,830, suggesting the cash market will open modestly higher. The contract ended Tuesday at a 17-point premium to cash, continuing to signal a positive near-term bias, amid the Hong Kong market's 8th day higher in the past 10 sessions. January open interest increases by 3,577 lots to 98,961. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI rises 0.2% to 3246.36 with 22 of the 30 index components trading higher after Wall Street's gain Tuesday, and amid broadly higher regional markets; there are over 3 gainers per decliner with 361 million shares traded on the broad market. From a regional perspective, Daiwa says it expects ASEAN markets to build on their gains for 2010 in 2011, "as corporate earnings ride on robust household spendi