\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/TWD Down On Stk Inflows; Support 29.050-Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 02:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD drifts lower to 29.097, compared to the 29.19-29.22 band most of yesterday. A trader from a foreign bank notes strong flows to the stock market; the Taiex is last +0.6%, extending yesterday's 1.3% gain. He tips immediate support for the pair at 29.050, and expects it's unlikely to break below the key 29.00 support ahead of the Lunar New Year holidays which start Feb. 2. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher, led by coal miners and metal firms due to rising overseas oil and metal prices on supply concerns and a weakened U.S. dollar. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.4% at 2814.62, with analysts tipping immediate resistance at 2850 today. "In the absence of fresh negative news, cheaply-valued blue chips would be attractive and will curb any downside of the market in the short term," Huatai Securities says in a note, adding some financial stocks and property developers, such as \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ), may outperform as the earning season approaches. Among advancers, coal miner Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 3.5% at CNY28.91 and China Coal (601898.SH) rises 1.5% to CNY11.05. Metal firm Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) gains 1.4% to CNY43.25 and Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) advances 0.7% to CNY48.80. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.5% at 1257.56. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares should bounce back today after 3 days of declines, which has included heavy foreign net selling; mild gains on Wall Street overnight along with a slightly more positive tone on regional markets will give sentiment a bit of a boost. KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul says investors should keep an eye on the Portugal bond sale tonight to get a better indication of European sentiment in the coming days; he adds that the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  should lift its policy rate by 25 bps to 2.25% today, which will be positive for equity inflows. BOT's policy rate decision and statement is due at 0730 GMT. Resistance is at 1025, with support at 1000. The index ends down 0.5% at 1013.39. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's smaller-than-expected current account surplus for November shows that the country's overseas shipments lost more steam near the end of last year, dragging on the export-driven economy, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute. "Exports to Asia and the U.S. lost momentum, and though the strong yen on a whole isn't uniformly bad it did weigh on exports and eat into overseas income brought back to Japan," he says. But in 2011, Kusaba says there are signs that demand for Japanese goods from China, the U.S. and other key markets is likely to increase as those economies strengthen, which could prevent sharper declines in exports, and limit the overall economic impact ahead. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The strong yen may have been a factor in China's net sale of Y81.3 billion of Japan financial assets in November, most of which are thought to be JGBs, as the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have prompted Beijing to take profits on its government bond holdings, says Hirokata Kusaba, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute, who adds, no sharp selloff is expected ahead. China bought a net Y262.5 billion of Japanese financial assets in October, according to current account data from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kusaba says as long as China continues to keep the CNY on a leash, which requires it to regularly buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the Chinese unit from rising, it will likely continue trending toward net JPY asset purchases. "There has been no change to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  constantly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so even though we may see some Chinese JGB selling to lock in profits when the yen rises, that doesn't mean China will be dumping Japanese bonds ahead." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] January HSI futures are up 0.2% at 23,830, suggesting the cash market will open modestly higher. The contract ended Tuesday at a 17-point premium to cash, continuing to signal a positive near-term bias, amid the Hong Kong market's 8th day higher in the past 10 sessions. January open interest increases by 3,577 lots to 98,961. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI rises 0.2% to 3246.36 with 22 of the 30 index components trading higher after Wall Street's gain Tuesday, and amid broadly higher regional markets; there are over 3 gainers per decliner with 361 million shares traded on the broad market. From a regional perspective, Daiwa says it expects ASEAN markets to build on their gains for 2010 in 2011, "as corporate earnings ride on robust household spending and rising infrastructure investment." Though it says real-GDP and EPS growth may slow this year, "we see early signs that economic growth is broadening, which we believe bodes well for the medium-term." The house remains Neutral on Singapore in the region as "relative valuations look less compelling than for ASEAN on average." Analysts tip STI's next resistance at 3250, support at 3220. \uc2 Straits Asia Resources\uc2  (AJ1.SG) surges 7.2% to S$2.83 as investors react positively to news it was issued the Principle Licence for the Northern Leases coal mining area; Hyflux (600.SG) gains 2.1% to S$2.39 on project wins in China. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW extends its fall and is testing the 1,120 support amid selling by offshore players; the pair is now at 1,120.40 compared to 1,125.10 late yesterday in Seoul. A rebound in the EUR/USD and foreign buying in local stocks are all helping lift the local unit, says a local bank trader. He expects the USD/KRW to hover around 1,120 for now; he adds "there doesn't appear to be any (BOK) smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, but the chances are for them to kick in later" in support of USD. The trader pegs next support at 1,118. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan banking and brokerage shares are outperforming the broader market on easing concerns over European debt problems and a general return in bullish market sentiment after the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose Tuesday for the 1st time in 4 sessions. Sumitomo Mitsui FG (8316.TO) is up 3.2% at Y3,015, recovering to the Y3,000 level for the first time since early May. Mitsubishi UFJ FG (8306.TO) is also up 3.4% at Y457. \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 is up 3.7% at Y169--its highest level since May 28. "Buying appetite among foreign investors is strong as they begin to price in European debt concerns," says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. Among brokerages, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) is up 2.6% at Y550, off its high of Y552 (and highest level since June 16). Daiwa Securities (8601.TO) is also up 1.4% at Y446. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY is down as worries about the economic impact of the floods in Australia continue to weigh, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior analyst Teppei Ino. The AUD/JPY is now at 82.05, off an earlier low at 81.73, from 82.18 late yesterday in New York. He says AUD selling will likely accelerate if Australian jobs data for December due tomorrow come in worse than expected, "since they believe the January figures can only be higher" due to the floods that began in late-December. A Dow Jones poll tips the December unemployment rate at 5.2%, unchanged from November. Ino pegs near-term support for the AUD/JPY at 81.00, and if that level is breached, the cross may fall to 80.50 by the end of this week. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.4% at 1568.42 on broad-based gains, with property stocks leading the way (subindex up 0.7%), says a local brokerage dealer; he expects the index to test 1575 in the near term given sustained buying momentum. The market breadth positive with gainers outpacing losers four to one. TA Research says lower liners should remain well-support on robust rotational plays, with positive news flow such as M&A and contract awards, likely to boost stocks in the construction, property, plantation and oil and gas sectors. However, the index should remain range bound below immediate resistance at 1575, and the 1.5 Fibonacci Projection target, says TA. Among gainers, SP Setia (8664.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR6.60, \uc2 Mah Sing\uc2  (8583.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR2.13, Dialog (7277.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR2.22 and CIMB (1023.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR8.84. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower at 7.7760 vs 7.7787 late Tuesday, tracking the USD's broad weakness against the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a Singapore bank trader. "There is no clear direction for the Hong Kong dollar as the market focuses on euro now," with investors awaiting the results of Portugal's debt auction later in the global day. He expects the pair to trade in tight 7.7740-7.7790 range today. (fiona.law@dowjones.com) [ 01-11-11 2058ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110112e71c0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
