s, likely to boost stocks in the construction, property, plantation and oil and gas sectors. However, the index should remain range bound below immediate resistance at 1575, and the 1.5 Fibonacci Projection target, says TA. Among gainers, SP Setia (8664.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR6.60, \uc2 Mah Sing\uc2  (8583.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR2.13, Dialog (7277.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR2.22 and CIMB (1023.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR8.84. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower at 7.7760 vs 7.7787 late Tuesday, tracking the USD's broad weakness against the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a Singapore bank trader. "There is no clear direction for the Hong Kong dollar as the market focuses on euro now," with investors awaiting the results of Portugal's debt auction later in the global day. He expects the pair to trade in tight 7.7740-7.7790 range today. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is steady at 9,060 vs 9,065 late yesterday as fund outflows seem to have ebbed. "Gains in most regional bourses so far bring hope that Indonesian stocks will be in positive territory today," a dealer says; note, the pair's recent rise was mostly attributed to outflows from the local stock market. Two dealers suspect Bank Indonesia to have offered the greenback at IDR9,070 earlier but there were no takers. The pair is tipped in a 9,045-9,085 range for the day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds are mixed in early trade with rate hike concerns weighing, particularly on the shorter-end bonds, ahead of the Bank of Korea's rate-setting meeting Thursday, analysts say. The 3-year yield is quoted +2 bps at 3.62%, the 5-year yield is quoted down 1 bp at 4.25%, while the 10-year yield is down 4 bps at 4.66%. March futures are 4 ticks higher at 102.99. "I still see a low chance of a January rate hike. But hawkish remarks are expected following the BOK rate review, and we can't completely rule out the possibility of a surprise rate hike," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2  analyst Hwang Soo-ho. 11 of 15 economists and fixed-income analysts surveyed by Dow Jones predict the BOK will keep its key rate at 2.50%, while the remaining 4 forecast a 25-bp hike to 2.75%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI may rise but could hover around the 23,800 level, as the Dow Jones Industrial Average's modest gains overnight may not provide strong direction for the local bourse, says Castor Pang, research director at \uc2 Cinda International\uc2 . He tips a 24,000 immediate cap for the index. "Trading sentiment is expected to remain a bit mixed amid the ongoing European debt concerns, and while we assess the potential impact on the equities market from news of the program for Wenzhou residents to invest overseas." The government of Wenzhou, a city in eastern China's Zhejiang province, said Friday it launched a pilot program that allows residents to invest directly overseas, but residents aren't allowed to invest in overseas property or equities markets under the trial. The HSI ended up 1.0% at 23,760.34 Tuesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian housing finance data continues to improve with Scott Haslem, chief economist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  saying the market has now officially troughed. For November, the number of owner-occupier loans rose 2.5% marking the fifth consecutive rise; analysts had expected a 1.0% decline. "There is certainly evidence here that housing lending has troughed, with renewed activity - particularly in the owner-occupier and 'new' market - over the past couple of months, with non-bank lenders getting an increasing share of this activity," says Haslem. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com) [ 01-11-11 2053ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110112e71c0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100