\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(12/24) Yuan's Role Grows in Hong Kong\b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2010\par 03:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WSJ(12/24) Yuan's Role Grows in Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) By Javier E. David\par \par \uc2 Six months after China pledged to increase the flexibility of the yuan exchange rate versus the dollar, there are fresh signs that Beijng is gradually loosening its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan rose to its highest level against the dollar in a month on Thursday after the People's Bank of China adjusted its daily target exchange rate upward. Traders took that as a signal that China would allow a faster rate of appreciation in coming weeks as President Hu Jintao prepares for a visit to Washington in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has risen 2.8% against the dollar since June 19, when China ended its rigid peg to the dollar. However, U.S. producers complain that the yuan remains far undervalued and that by actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it from rising, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives its manufacturers an unfair advantage.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury has voiced its concerns to Beijing about its policy, but for the most part takes a hands-off approach.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of China's approach say that the yuan's current rate of appreciation is more or less in line with that which occurred in the three years before it was fixed during the 2008 crisis, when it gained 20% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also Thursday, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said yuan deposits in the city surged to 280 billion yuan ($42.12 billion) at the end of November from 217 billion yuan in the previous month, a sign of strong demand for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hong Kong. HKMA Chief Executive Norman Chan said the yuan market is expected to "develop progressively" in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Hong Kong banks introduced a technical change that will make more use of electronic settlement in financial transactions that use the yuan. The primary purpose of the yuan-deposit program, which was introduced this year, is to facilitate trade. But it is also seen as an important early step toward the full internationalization of the yuan, which would eventually involve making it convertible for capital transactions.\par \par \uc2 Economic developments in the U.S. -- including a widening budget deficit and loose monetary policy from the Federal Reserve -- have spurred China's determination to diversify its $2.65 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, most of which are in U.S dollars.\par \par \uc2 Fears of a potential bond-bubble bursting, partly stoked by a recent jump in Treasury yields, are also seen as encouraging China to turn the yuan into an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in turn reduce the need to accumulate dollars.\par \par \uc2 But capital-account convertibility would make the yuan subject to the vagaries of market forces. Currently, China uses rigorous capital controls to dissuade speculative flows. Most analysts don't see that changing anytime soon, especially as the government wages a battle against surging inflation.\par \par \uc2 With China also now fostering a yuan-denominated bond market in Hong Kong, its latest steps toward liberalization have surprised some observers with how open it is becoming to adjusting the existing regime. And yet by far the biggest changes are still to come.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody would have predicted this 10 years ago," the Cato Institute's Mr. Dorn said. "This is the tip of the iceberg."\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020101225e6co00107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
