\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Taiwan dlr, won up as Irish bail-out lifts stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2010\par 09:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2  * Taiwan dlr gains up to 1.5 pct; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spotted    \tab \par  * Won at 1-week high; ignores \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tightening, N.Korea    \tab \par      \tab \par  By Cheon Jong-woo    \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The Taiwan dollar and the South Korean won led overall gains in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday after Europe and the IMF agreed to help Ireland with its debt problems, which supported a return to riskier assets such as Asian stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Officials will begin thrashing out details of the three-year bailout package -- expected to total 80 billion to 90 billion euros -- later on Monday, with loans to tackle its banking and budget crisis in a bid to protect Europe's financial stability. [ID:nLDE6AK039]\par \par \uc2 Still, investors remained worried about possible contagion in Europe, and uncertainty over size and condition of the aid to Ireland capped gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to stay in a range. All are watching the Europe where lots of statements are coming out on Irish bail-out," said a dealer in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players in Asia also stayed wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities and more steps to reduce strong fund flows which have been supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar rose as much as 1.5 percent against the U.S. dollar on modest volume, but gave up some of its earlier gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's was again spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its rise.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen defending the level of 30.25 per dollar, IFR Markets reported. [ID:nIFR8ZWyF]\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won hit a one-week high against the dollar as foreign investors kept buying local shares and on exporters' demand for settlements.\par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors ignored further bank reserve increases from China tightening late on Friday. [ID:nL3E6MJ0N8]\par \par \uc2 Instead, they remained wary of possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities and more measures to curb hot money inflows, dealers and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Cautious trade is expected to strengthen a resistance line of the won at 1,120 per dollar, analysts and dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "The won has more room to rise, probably to 1,120 per dollar, but I doubt if it would strengthen past the level on lingering risks of the authorities," said Jeong My-young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Samsung Futures Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to 1,124.2, the strongest since Nov. 15 as shipbuilders and other exporters chased it for settlements.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit rose 0.19 percent to 3.1070 per dollar in thin trade as investors largely viewed the Irish request for aid as positive for the euro.\par \par \uc2 "Those that went long USD last week closing out those positions," said another Kuala Lumpur-based trader. Some traders believe China inflation fears have been factored into the market.\par \par \uc2 Traders generally expected slow, thin trade for the rest of this week ahead of the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday on Thursday. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0705 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\tab \par  Japan yen        83.44          83.45       +0.01\tab \par  Sing dlr        1.2941         1.2960       +0.15\tab \par  Taiwan dlr      30.235         30.673       +1.45\tab \par  Korean won     1125.50        1133.60       +0.72\tab \par  Baht             29.92          29.92       +0.00\tab \par  Peso             43.90          43.83       -0.16\tab \par  Rupiah         8927.00        8930.00       +0.03\tab \par  Rupee            45.27          45.29       +0.04\tab \par  Ringgit         3.1070         3.1130       +0.19\tab \par  Yuan            6.6405         6.6395       -0.02\tab \par  \tab \par  Change so far in 2010\tab \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\tab \par  Japan yen        83.44          92.90      +11.34\tab \par  Sing dlr        1.2941         1.4053       +8.59\tab \par  Taiwan dlr      30.235         32.030       +5.94\tab \par  Korean won     1125.50        1164.50       +3.47\tab \par  Baht             29.92          33.32      +11.36\tab \par  Peso             43.90          46.20       +5.24\tab \par  Rupiah         8927.00        9420.00       +5.52\tab \par  Rupee            45.27          46.54       +2.81\tab \par  Ringgit         3.1070         3.4220      +10.14\tab \par  Yuan            6.6405         6.8270       +2.81\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Clement Tan in SINGAPORE and Peter Whitley from IFR Markets; Editing by Chris Lewis) For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight