\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: PBOC Gov: Emerging Markets Face Capital Inflow Pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2010\par 06:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from PBOC governor on inflation and outstanding bonds, background on October CPI growth.)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Emerging markets are facing pressure from capital inflows and China plans to make its monetary policy more flexible to resolve problems in its economy, People's Bank of China Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan said Tuesday, highlighting the Chinese government's concerns over rising inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 Zhou's comments come after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator announced measures last week to crack down further on hot money inflows, including strict quotas for short-term foreign debt issued by financial institutions, due to concerns the Federal Reserve's latest quantitative easing will lead to excess global liquidity and higher asset prices. China also raised banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 basis points effective Tuesday, the fourth increase this year, to contain excess liquidity and inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 'The economic recovery in developed nations is slowing, resulting in continuously loose monetary conditions,' Zhou said at a financial forum in Beijing. 'Some emerging markets are facing certain pressure of capital inflows,' he said.\par \par \uc2 'China also face some problems in its economy, such as the need to continue to boost residents' income and improve the structure of the economy, while all industries need to pay attention to rising price pressures,' Zhou said.\par \par \uc2 China's consumer price index rose 4.4% in October, its highest increase in two years, following September's gain of 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 Zhou also said China's bond market can't currently satisfy the domestic economy's funding needs, and the government should reduce administrative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly controls corporate bond sales and limits the amount companies can raise.\par \par \uc2 Outstanding debt in the domestic market at the end of October rose 21% from a year earlier to CNY20.5 trillion, Zhou said.\par \par \uc2 He also reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to strengthen macro-economic controls, improve the efficiency and flexibility of China's monetary policy and maintain appropriate growth of the money supply. Zhou also repeated China will steadily push forward the reform of the country's interest-rate regime and improve the yuan exchange-rate mechanism.\par \par \uc2 -Rose Yu and Wang Ming contributed to this article, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; rose.yu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020101116e6bg0070u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
