\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HK Mortage Mkt Competition To Stay Intense -JPM\b0\par\par\uc2 1,818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2010\par 08:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes mortgage rate increases marginally in HK, but house expects fundamental impact to banks' earnings to be insignificant (less than 1%). "We believe the aim of this move is to relieve some of the pressure on net interest margin as a result of the popularity of HIBOR-based mortgages (90.5% of new mortgages) and in anticipation of a lower HIBOR for a prolonged period underpinned by the monetary policy of the U.S." Expects competition in mortgage market to remain intense in 2011, given commoditized nature, potential liquidity inflow, and aspiration of Chinese banks in domestic banking market. Among HK banks, BOC HK (2388.HK) down 0.9% at HK$26.75, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of East Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0023.HK) +0.3% at HK$33.60, Hang Seng Bank (0011.HK) +1.5% at HK$123.00. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0702 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 1.5% at 23,660.51, extending losses vs 0.1% fall mid-day, sentiment weighed by further losses in China markets (Shanghai Composite last down 3.7%) after PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan says China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve problems in domestic economy, such as rising capital inflows. Prudential associate director Alvin Cheung expects index may have 23,000 as near-term support; "more trading pressure is expected in the near-term on concerns of more potential tightening measures in China." HKEx (0388.HK) down 1.4% at HK$181.60 amid broader market's decline, though \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) +3.9% at HK$22.90 after company forecasts record FY10 net profit of at least HK$12.50 billion. Volume modest at HK$63.61 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF's close above its 55-day moving average for the first time since mid-June signals upside potential is now open to the parity level, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "This should be enough to initiate further upside interest to the psychologically important 1.00 level, which is also the Fibonacci retracement," says technical analyst Karen Anne Jones. "This will act as the break point to 1.0329," she notes. USD/CHF at 0.9852 by 0633 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF holds above 0.9800 but is showing few signs of being able to extend these gains, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Whether the latest move in the USD/CHF will have enough stamina to push the pair through the key resistance of 99.72, and eventually back above parity, remains to be seen," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. USD/CHF at 0.9850 by 0625 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex extends losses, now down 1.9% at 19,919.04 tracking negative cues from Chinese markets, with foreign funds unloading shares. "There is selling pressure on weak cues from China on fears that foreign investors will sell even more aggressively. Still, I don't expect the Sensex to close below the 19,900 level," says local dealer. Adds, Shanghai Composite Index down 3.4% at 2913.31 after PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan says China plans to make monetary policy more flexible to resolve problems inflationary pressures in domestic economy from rising capital inflows. Support tipped at intraday low of 19,852.50. Among actives, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) off 3.25 at INR1212, \uc2 Larsen & Toubro\uc2  (500510.BY) off 3% at INR2018.95, ITC (500875.BY) off 2.6% at INR170.20 and \uc2 Infosys Technologies\uc2  (500209.BY) off 2.1% at INR2962. (john.kumar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.6% at 1492.63, tracking declines in regional bourses, says local brokerage dealer. "The weak Chinese markets are making people jittery and most of them opted to clear positions ahead of Wednesday's public holiday," says dealer. TA Research says blue chips should extend profit-taking consolidation in near-term as trading momentum dwindles due to cautious market undertone. Tips key KLCI support at last Friday's low of 1488, which coincides with 50% Fibonacci Retracement of the rise from the pivot low of 1445 on Sept. 24 to Nov. 10's all-time high of 1532. Among gainers, \uc2 Petronas Dagangan\uc2  (5681.KU) +1.1% at MYR11.00, Amway (6351.KU) +3.0% at MYR8.49 and Tan Chong (4405.KU) +1.3% at MYR5.54. On the downside, Timedotcom (5031.KU) down 15.6% to MYR0.65 on planned capital reduction, Bursa (1818.KU) down 1.1% at MYR8.31 and UEM Land (5148.KU) down 4.8% at MYR2.19. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier 1,124.9 low as EUR comes off earlier high. 4 Seoul-based traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought USD near 1,126 in late trade; one of those traders estimate suspected buying worth around $300 million. Pair tad down late at 1,129.5 vs 1,131.9 yesterday following BOK's 25-bp rate hike. "It seems the authorities don't want to let the pair fall (sharply) from the rate hike," says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 1,110-1,140 range for rest of week. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR may keep weakening even if Ireland files request for EU bailout, as move might only ease market's worries over region's debt issues for short period, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "If Ireland is helped, investors will just look for the next (country in trouble)," he says. Adds, next likely target includes Greece, as its prime minister recently said possibility of taking some extra time to repay its bailout loans "already on the table." Nishii says speculation increasing that Greece can't avoid default. EUR/USD last at 1.3614, next big support 1.350, dealers say.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakness in China bourses taking toll on Singapore shares in post-lunch trade. Market breadth turns negative from slight positive at end of morning session. STI down 0.8% at 3211.52 vs flat at 3235.06 midday, with immediate support at 3200. "We have had such a good rally (in recent months). I expect some short-term profit-taking but because of QE2, we are still relatively overweight on equities. Take the opportunity to accumulate," says ABN Amro Private Bank's Asia research head Daphne Roth. Key decliners among STI stocks include \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 3.1% at S$3.16, SIA Engineering (S59.SG), off 2.7% at S$4.33, Olam (O32.SG), off 2.5% at S$3.10, \uc2 Fraser & Neave\uc2  (F99.SG), off 1.8% at S$6.39. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.3%; S&P/ASX +0.3%; HSI down 0.1%: Kospi down 0.8%; Taiex +0.9%; Sensex down 0.6%; Shanghai Composite down 3.7%; Indonesian shares +0.6%; Philippine closed; SET flat%; NZX-50 down 0.4%; STI down 0.1%; KLCI +0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR stages mild recovery after falling to 7-week low vs USD earlier in session as investors focused on whether Ireland will formally request emergency funding from EU when region's finance ministers meet this week. EUR/USD at 1.3614 vs 1.3587 in late NY on Monday, EUR/JPY at 112.93 vs 113.00, USD/JPY at 82.96 vs 83.17. NY Fed President Dudley says Treasury buying equivalent to 75 bps rate cut, will have modest, positive effect on economy. Fed vice chairwoman Yellen says in WSJ interview would seriously consider more Treasury purchases if economy remains weak. Adds, Fed doesn't want inflation above 2.0%, sees unemployment above 8.0% through 2012. China October FDI +7.9% on-year at $7.66 billion vs +6.1% on-year in September. PBOC Governor Zhou reiterates China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve economic problems. Adds, should reduce administrative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bond market. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to launch trading of Russian ruble against CNY in onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market according to China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. BOK raises key policy rate 25 bps to 2.50%. BOK removes phrase "keeping accommodative stance" from policy statement; BOK Governor says removal of phrase doesn't suggest continued tightening. RBA says in meeting minutes November hike decision finely balanced, risks suggested hike was prudent. BOT official says no need yet for additional measures to curb THB's strength, current level of capital inflows is okay. Singapore October non-oil exports +34.5% on-year vs 22.6% revised in September, +25.9% expected. Spot gold at $1359.80/oz, down 70 cents vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures down 44 cents at $84.42/bbl.(john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.9% at 8312.21, rebounds from 3-day losing streak, led by gains in tech heavyweights, but in relatively thin volume of NT$87.00 billion; HTC (2498.TW) +2.3% at NT$797.00, Hon Hai (2317.TW) +0.9% at NT$113.00, TSMC (2330.TW) +0.9% at NT$64.50. Andrew Teng, analyst at Taiwan International Securities, says today's gains "appear to lack conviction. Further confirmation will be needed for a possible trend reversal." Tips Taiex to stay in consolidation mode near-term with strong support at 60-day moving average of 8100, "with a slightly downward bias for the week given the resurgence of European debt issues." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD, NZD, EUR fall vs JPY, while EUR/USD also drops as risk aversion rises on weak Asian shares, say senior dealers at major bank in Tokyo. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average closed down 0.3%, while Shanghai Composite Index losing more than 3%. Adds EUR/USD's next technical level at 1.3500, where many stop-loss selling orders placed. Focus now on Ireland whether it will seek help during euro-zone finance ministers' meeting; "If there are no requests (for financial help) from Ireland by tomorrow, I expect the euro will decline to $1.3500," Yuji Saito at Credit Agricole says. Another dealer tips NZD/JPY last at 64.04, may fall to 63.50. AUD/JPY now at 81.65, may fall to 81.00. EUR/JPY at 112.89, may fall to 112.50. EUR/USD at 1.3614, immediate support at 1.36.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 11-16-10 0216ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101116e6bg0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
