bishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "If Ireland is helped, investors will just look for the next (country in trouble)," he says. Adds, next likely target includes Greece, as its prime minister recently said possibility of taking some extra time to repay its bailout loans "already on the table." Nishii says speculation increasing that Greece can't avoid default. EUR/USD last at 1.3614, next big support 1.350, dealers say.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakness in China bourses taking toll on Singapore shares in post-lunch trade. Market breadth turns negative from slight positive at end of morning session. STI down 0.8% at 3211.52 vs flat at 3235.06 midday, with immediate support at 3200. "We have had such a good rally (in recent months). I expect some short-term profit-taking but because of QE2, we are still relatively overweight on equities. Take the opportunity to accumulate," says ABN Amro Private Bank's Asia research head Daphne Roth. Key decliners among STI stocks include \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 3.1% at S$3.16, SIA Engineering (S59.SG), off 2.7% at S$4.33, Olam (O32.SG), off 2.5% at S$3.10, \uc2 Fraser & Neave\uc2  (F99.SG), off 1.8% at S$6.39. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.3%; S&P/ASX +0.3%; HSI down 0.1%: Kospi down 0.8%; Taiex +0.9%; Sensex down 0.6%; Shanghai Composite down 3.7%; Indonesian shares +0.6%; Philippine closed; SET flat%; NZX-50 down 0.4%; STI down 0.1%; KLCI +0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR stages mild recovery after falling to 7-week low vs USD earlier in session as investors focused on whether Ireland will formally request emergency funding from EU when region's finance ministers meet this week. EUR/USD at 1.3614 vs 1.3587 in late NY on Monday, EUR/JPY at 112.93 vs 113.00, USD/JPY at 82.96 vs 83.17. NY Fed President Dudley says Treasury buying equivalent to 75 bps rate cut, will have modest, positive effect on economy. Fed vice chairwoman Yellen says in WSJ interview would seriously consider more Treasury purchases if economy remains weak. Adds, Fed doesn't want inflation above 2.0%, sees unemployment above 8.0% through 2012. China October FDI +7.9% on-year at $7.66 billion vs +6.1% on-year in September. PBOC Governor Zhou reiterates China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve economic problems. Adds, should reduce administrative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bond market. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to launch trading of Russian ruble against CNY in onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market according to China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. BOK raises key policy rate 25 bps to 2.50%. BOK removes phrase "keeping accommodative stance" from policy statement; BOK Governor says removal of phrase doesn't suggest continued tightening. RBA says in meeting minutes November hike decision finely balanced, risks suggested hike was prudent. BOT official says no need yet for additional measures to curb THB's strength, current level of capital inflows is okay. Singapore October non-oil exports +34.5% on-year vs 22.6% revised in September, +25.9% expected. Spot gold at $1359.80/oz, down 70 cents vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures down 44 cents at $84.42/bbl.(john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.9% at 8312.21, rebounds from 3-day losing streak, led by gains in tech heavyweights, but in relatively thin volume of NT$87.00 billion; HTC (2498.TW) +2.3% at NT$797.00, Hon Hai (2317.TW) +0.9% at NT$113.00, TSMC (2330.TW) +0.9% at NT$64.50. Andrew Teng, analyst at Taiwan International Securities, says today's gains "appear to lack conviction. Further confirmation will be needed for a possible trend reversal." Tips Taiex to stay in consolidation mode near-term with strong support at 60-day moving average of 8100, "with a slightly downward bias for the week given the resurgence of European debt issues." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD, NZD, EUR fall vs JPY, while EUR/USD also drops as risk aversion rises on weak Asian shares, say senior dealers at major bank in Tokyo. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average closed down 0.3%, while Shanghai Composite Index losing more than 3%. Adds EUR/USD's next technical level at 1.3500, where many stop-loss selling orders placed. Focus now on Ireland whether it will seek help during euro-zone finance ministers' meeting; "If there are no requests (for financial help) from Ireland by tomorrow, I expect the euro will decline to $1.3500," Yuji Saito at Credit Agricole says. Another dealer tips NZD/JPY last at 64.04, may fall to 63.50. AUD/JPY now at 81.65, may fall to 81.00. EUR/JPY at 112.89, may fall to 112.50. EUR/USD at 1.3614, immediate support at 1.36.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses as investors perceive policy risks are increasing, after PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan says China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve problems in domestic economy, such as rising capital inflows. Shanghai Composite Index down 3.5% at 2909.61; may test support at psychologically key 2900 level. "It could be Zhou's comments giving markets the jitters though we earlier expect the market to consolidate around the 2900 level after the strong gains in recent weeks," says Zhang Gang analyst from Central China Securities; adds, concerns over another possible interest rate hike still persisting. \uc2 Datong Coal Industry Co\uc2 . (601001.SH) off 7.7% at CNY20.99, \uc2 Inner Mongolia Baotou Steel Rare-Earth (Group) Hi-Tech\uc2  Co. (600111.SH) off 8.0% at CNY74.00, \uc2 China Construction Bank Corp\uc2 . (601939.SH) off 4.3% at CNY4.88, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) off 3.9% at CNY8.20. Shenzhen Composite Index down 3.4% at 1283.56. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 46.9% at HK$2.622 billion midday, as percentage of total market volume unchanged vs Monday's half-day at 5.3%, suggesting view HSI's near-term downside may be limited after 2.7% fall Friday-Monday. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) leads with HK$313.51 million, followed by Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$158.15 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$128.92 million. Then come \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$126.83 million, Bank of China (3988.HK) with HK$107.16 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 11-16-10 0200ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101116e6bg00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
