\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian Summary\b0\par\par\uc2 1,787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2010\par 07:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 0.3%; S&P/ASX +0.3%; HSI down 0.1%: Kospi down 0.8%; Taiex +0.9%; Sensex down 0.6%; Shanghai Composite down 3.7%; Indonesian shares +0.6%; Philippine closed; SET flat%; NZX-50 down 0.4%; STI down 0.1%; KLCI +0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR stages mild recovery after falling to 7-week low vs USD earlier in session as investors focused on whether Ireland will formally request emergency funding from EU when region's finance ministers meet this week. EUR/USD at 1.3614 vs 1.3587 in late NY on Monday, EUR/JPY at 112.93 vs 113.00, USD/JPY at 82.96 vs 83.17. NY Fed President Dudley says Treasury buying equivalent to 75 bps rate cut, will have modest, positive effect on economy. Fed vice chairwoman Yellen says in WSJ interview would seriously consider more Treasury purchases if economy remains weak. Adds, Fed doesn't want inflation above 2.0%, sees unemployment above 8.0% through 2012. China October FDI +7.9% on-year at $7.66 billion vs +6.1% on-year in September. PBOC Governor Zhou reiterates China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve economic problems. Adds, should reduce administrative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bond market. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to launch trading of Russian ruble against CNY in onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market according to China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. BOK raises key policy rate 25 bps to 2.50%. BOK removes phrase "keeping accommodative stance" from policy statement; BOK Governor says removal of phrase doesn't suggest continued tightening. RBA says in meeting minutes November hike decision finely balanced, risks suggested hike was prudent. BOT official says no need yet for additional measures to curb THB's strength, current level of capital inflows is okay. Singapore October non-oil exports +34.5% on-year vs 22.6% revised in September, +25.9% expected. Spot gold at $1359.80/oz, down 70 cents vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures down 44 cents at $84.42/bbl.(john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.9% at 8312.21, rebounds from 3-day losing streak, led by gains in tech heavyweights, but in relatively thin volume of NT$87.00 billion; HTC (2498.TW) +2.3% at NT$797.00, Hon Hai (2317.TW) +0.9% at NT$113.00, TSMC (2330.TW) +0.9% at NT$64.50. Andrew Teng, analyst at Taiwan International Securities, says today's gains "appear to lack conviction. Further confirmation will be needed for a possible trend reversal." Tips Taiex to stay in consolidation mode near-term with strong support at 60-day moving average of 8100, "with a slightly downward bias for the week given the resurgence of European debt issues." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD, NZD, EUR fall vs JPY, while EUR/USD also drops as risk aversion rises on weak Asian shares, say senior dealers at major bank in Tokyo. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average closed down 0.3%, while Shanghai Composite Index losing more than 3%. Adds EUR/USD's next technical level at 1.3500, where many stop-loss selling orders placed. Focus now on Ireland whether it will seek help during euro-zone finance ministers' meeting; "If there are no requests (for financial help) from Ireland by tomorrow, I expect the euro will decline to $1.3500," Yuji Saito at Credit Agricole says. Another dealer tips NZD/JPY last at 64.04, may fall to 63.50. AUD/JPY now at 81.65, may fall to 81.00. EUR/JPY at 112.89, may fall to 112.50. EUR/USD at 1.3614, immediate support at 1.36.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses as investors perceive policy risks are increasing, after PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan says China plans to increase policy flexibility to resolve problems in domestic economy, such as rising capital inflows. Shanghai Composite Index down 3.5% at 2909.61; may test support at psychologically key 2900 level. "It could be Zhou's comments giving markets the jitters though we earlier expect the market to consolidate around the 2900 level after the strong gains in recent weeks," says Zhang Gang analyst from Central China Securities; adds, concerns over another possible interest rate hike still persisting. \uc2 Datong Coal Industry Co\uc2 . (601001.SH) off 7.7% at CNY20.99, \uc2 Inner Mongolia Baotou Steel Rare-Earth (Group) Hi-Tech\uc2  Co. (600111.SH) off 8.0% at CNY74.00, \uc2 China Construction Bank Corp\uc2 . (601939.SH) off 4.3% at CNY4.88, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) off 3.9% at CNY8.20. Shenzhen Composite Index down 3.4% at 1283.56. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 46.9% at HK$2.622 billion midday, as percentage of total market volume unchanged vs Monday's half-day at 5.3%, suggesting view HSI's near-term downside may be limited after 2.7% fall Friday-Monday. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) leads with HK$313.51 million, followed by Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$158.15 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$128.92 million. Then come \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$126.83 million, Bank of China (3988.HK) with HK$107.16 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares last down 0.2% at 1027.03 midday pause in choppy trade, robust volume. Small, medium-cap stocks with individual cues dominating market, while banking stocks lower, mostly ignoring Moody's Investors Service announcement that outlook for Thai banking system stable over next 12-18 months. Thai stock market likely softer through this week but buying in tax-saving mutual funds as approaching year-end may help limit downside, says Capital Nomura Securities strategist Chai Jirasevenupraphund. Support pegged at 1015-1018, resistance at 1030-1035 band. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) off 0.8% at THB6, \uc2 Polyplex (Thailand)(\uc2 PTL.TH) off 5.4% at THB35, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.9% at THB163.50, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) steady at THB50, \uc2 Italian-Thai Development\uc2  (ITD.TH) +1.7% at THB4.94. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rate down but remains above \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 6.25% repo rate, says dealer with state-run bank; interbank call rate at 6.80% vs 6.85% last close, while collateralized borrowing and lending obligation or CBLO at 6.25% vs 6.30% at open. "Liquidity from IPO refunds is cornered by only a few banks which is keeping rates in overnight markets up. Banks have government securities to borrow at RBI's liquidity window," says dealer. Tips call rate to ease to 6.50% while CBLO likely in 6.10%-6.25% range. Reckons banks may borrow around INR1 trillion in 2 daily liquidity auctions. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's better-than-expected 5.8% on-month growth in October exports, seasonally adjusted, vs 4.7% contraction in September signals stronger growth in 2010; main challenge for next 12 months will be fighting inflation, Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew says. "While the October export performance reinforces the continued strong contribution towards Singapore's economy via external demand, the flipside will be that such strong growth rates will be hard to beat in 2011, especially if we expect the growth recovery in the U.S. to be lackluster with unemployment remaining high and consumer sentiment weakening," Liew says. Adds, "after the double dose of tightening on October 14, we expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep policy steady in April." (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slips more to test below 1,125 mark as EUR strengthens in late Asian hours, further boosting KRW sentiment on-top of hopes for more BOK tightening measures in future, traders say. Pair now down to 1,125.6 vs 1,127.7 earlier, 1,131.9 late yesterday in Seoul. But local bank trader says, given on-going concerns over possible measures to curb foreign capital, "I think the pair has pretty much fallen by all that it can for the day." Pegs support at 1,123. Adds, after bidding up USD in early trade, offshore players now showing mixture of buying, selling interest. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore dollar rises against USD after stronger-than-expected October exports growth at 34.5% on-year vs downwardly revised +22.6% in September, +25.9% expected in Dow Jones poll of 6 analysts. USD/SGD last at 1.2966 vs 1.2982 before government released data at 0500 GMT; pair earlier quoted as high as 1.3050. Trader tips support at 1.2950, resistance at 1.3050 for today. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Uncertainty over European sovereign debt problems, parallel sell off in US Treasurys, equities, direction of Chinese monetary policy all keeping LME metals subdued for now, says Tokyo based trader. "The feeling is that it's a bit of a chaotic situation right now so it's difficult to be confident," he says. LME metals all lower, with 3-month copper at $8,589/ton, down $55 in relatively quiet session by recent standards, with 1,538 lots done so far. LME 3-month zinc again underperforming, down $33 at $2,305/ton with 1,633 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0522 GMT [Dow Jones] USD falling vs EUR, JPY as investors reacting to dovish comments from New York Fed President William Dudley on CNBC appearance, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. "His remarks reaffirmed that the U.S. monetary policy will remain easy for possibly years," dealer says. Still, market impact from Dudley likely short-lived as he is known as one of most dovish members of board. "We know he is dovish, and he's been saying similar things in the recent past. So we don't take these comments too seriously," he says. Another Tokyo dealer says comments might have been reason to adjust positions. USD/JPY falls to 82.95 from 83.10 before comment, may fall to test 82.75. EUR/USD rises to 1.3641 from 1.3620, may rise to test 1.3660.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 11-16-10 0122ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101116e6bg00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
