sset purchase plan (so-called 'quantitative easing') should be reconsidered and discontinued." Pair last 82.93 v 82.75 prior to report. "the letter was used as excuse to push the dollar up by some Japanese investors," dealer in Tokyo bank says. Speculation mounting among dealers that Fed may scale back recently-announced easing measures if U.S. economic data keep logging strong figures. U.S. to release its retail sales data for October later in day. Dealers tip 1st resistance at 83.00, then 84.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0719 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds tad lower late in subdued trade, with mild selling in short-term paper after MYR weakens (USD/MYR last at 3.1300 vs 3.1090 late Friday), says local brokerage dealer. "In the absence of fresh leads, most investors opted to stay on the sidelines," says dealer; tips yields to move in tight 1-2 bp range tomorrow, ahead of public holiday Wednesday. Notes, no benchmark MGS traded yet. Yields on December 2010 GII +1 bp at 2.75% and June 2017 GII +1 bp at 3.76%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end flat in low volume as investors stay on sidelines ahead of PBOC's 1-year bill sale in regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  tomorrow. "Investors are focusing on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stabilize the one-year bill yield tomorrow after lifting the yield by 5 bps last week," says Beijing-based trader with local insurer. Adds, would be positive to bond prices if PBOC leaves bill yield unchanged, as such move might ease investors' concerns over possible interest rate hike. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 125.91, unchanged from last close.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues scaling higher as risk aversion resurfaces amid renewed concern on euro-zone debt problems, dealers say; pair now at 8,950 vs 8,923 late yesterday