\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Up, Some Dealers Cite WSJ Report As Cue\b0\par\par\uc2 1,557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2010\par 08:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0719 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY up, some dealers in Tokyo say investors taking cue from WSJ's open letter from a group of prominent Republican-leaning economists to Fed's Bernanke. In letter, economists say "we believe the Federal Reserve's large-scale asset purchase plan (so-called 'quantitative easing') should be reconsidered and discontinued." Pair last 82.93 v 82.75 prior to report. "the letter was used as excuse to push the dollar up by some Japanese investors," dealer in Tokyo bank says. Speculation mounting among dealers that Fed may scale back recently-announced easing measures if U.S. economic data keep logging strong figures. U.S. to release its retail sales data for October later in day. Dealers tip 1st resistance at 83.00, then 84.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0719 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds tad lower late in subdued trade, with mild selling in short-term paper after MYR weakens (USD/MYR last at 3.1300 vs 3.1090 late Friday), says local brokerage dealer. "In the absence of fresh leads, most investors opted to stay on the sidelines," says dealer; tips yields to move in tight 1-2 bp range tomorrow, ahead of public holiday Wednesday. Notes, no benchmark MGS traded yet. Yields on December 2010 GII +1 bp at 2.75% and June 2017 GII +1 bp at 3.76%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end flat in low volume as investors stay on sidelines ahead of PBOC's 1-year bill sale in regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  tomorrow. "Investors are focusing on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stabilize the one-year bill yield tomorrow after lifting the yield by 5 bps last week," says Beijing-based trader with local insurer. Adds, would be positive to bond prices if PBOC leaves bill yield unchanged, as such move might ease investors' concerns over possible interest rate hike. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 125.91, unchanged from last close.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues scaling higher as risk aversion resurfaces amid renewed concern on euro-zone debt problems, dealers say; pair now at 8,950 vs 8,923 late yesterday; three dealers suspect Bank Indonesia to have sold around $50 million near 8,950 to slow pair's rise. "Bank Indonesia didn't seem to be too vehement in curbing the dollar's rise perhaps because the dollar is still below the psychologically important level at 9,000," says one of dealers; tips 8,935-8,960 for rest of day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares end up, reversing earlier losses as uncertainty linked to further monetary tightening may be fading. Shanghai Composite Index ends +1.0% at 3014.41; faces resistance at psychologically important 3100 level. "Though choppy in the near-term, the liquidity-driven rally will likely carry on in the next six months. The combination of inflation, rate hikes, and yuan appreciation resembles that of early 2007, except that growth now is weaker and liquidity richer," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in a research note. Among actives, ICBC (601398.SH) +6.8% at CNY5.00, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) +0.2% at CNY12.95. Shenzhen Composite Index +2.4% at 1327.99. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI has already made it clear it wants to pause policy tightening for next few months, and October headline print is probably not enough to prompt re-assessment of "wait and see" approach, says Brian Jackson at \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 . Adds, it's clear inflation remains uncomfortably high despite the rate hikes already delivered, house continues to think RBI will have more work to do in 2011, forecasts repo rate to be increased another 75 bps. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1500.98 in afternoon trade after two consecutive sessions of declines. Dealer says, "the overall sentiment remains cautious. With the focus on European sovereign debt concerns, investors aren't willing to take aggressive positions." Adds, some trading in specific stocks, but investors largely staying on sidelines. Tips range-bound trade to continue rest of session, with 1495 support, 1513 (10-day moving average) resistance. Currently 393 losers vs 304 gainers. Genting (3182.KU) +1.8% at MYR10.34 on one-off gain; Tan Chong (4405.KU) +0.9% at MYR5.44 on vehicle manufacturing license in Sabah. Pacific & Orient (6009.KU) down 10.3% at MYR0.915 after talks to sell stake in non-life insurance unit Pacific & \uc2 Orient Insurance\uc2  to U.K. insurer \uc2 Prudential PLC\uc2  (PRU.LN) terminate. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwanese vice premier Sean Chen last week demanded Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  change way it manipulates TWD. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys massive quantities of USD from state-owned banks during last 15 minutes of onshore trading session. Makes only one or two trades, but large enough to bring value of TWD vs USD back down to level near previous day's close. So, should investors sell USD during trading hours? Or wait until onshore market closes to get better price? Problem is, investors literally can't sell USD at those "closing" prices. Banks in on the trade with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't pass the prices through to market because market expects TWD will continue to appreciate. Opening price for TWD vs USD on following day invariably lower than it would have been had \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the next day, but is higher than negotiated trading price. See =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Taiwan's Blunt \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 24,173.35, off 0.7% fall midday, after Shanghai Composite index reverses earlier losses, (last +0.2%), likely due to bargain-hunting interest after recent falls, says Castor Pang, research director at Cinda; tips HSI in 23,800-24,500 range near-term. "Some investors are hunting for bargains after recent falls, and I believe trading will remain volatile in the near-term." Adds, ample liquidity also helping limit further losses in local bourse. Suggests investors avoid China properties on rate hike expectations in China. Among China properties, China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 2.8% at HK$16, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 1.3% at HK$15.18. Volume lower vs recent average at HK$62.05 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.2% at 3246.58, off intraday 3230 low, which unlikely to be tested again today, with market breadth improving tad to 2.3 decliners per gainer vs almost 3 to 1 at break; 1.11 billion shares traded worth S$1.04 billion. Index likely to remain underwater rest of session as HK, China markets also consolidating with negative bias. "We're still looking for direction. There's plenty of data coming up which may give us a bit more clarity and direction, but this is a holiday-shortened week, so no one will be out there doing too much," says economist at local brokerage. Adds, it's only natural investors stepping back after earnings season, STI's rise to 34-month highs. Singapore markets closed Wednesday for holiday. REITs broadly lower: Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) off 1.4% at S$2.06, CCT (C61U.SG) down 2.0% at S$1.45. Rig builders buck trend; Keppel (BN4.SG) +0.2% at S$10.54, SembMarine (S51.SG) +0.6% at S$4.95. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip more on larger than expected supplementary government spending plan, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.19 vs 98.24 before news, 98.15 last close. "The market was expecting extra spending but this is probably a bit more than most would have expected," says trader. Says bonds likely to slip more as sticky prices, concerns on government's fiscal profligacy continue to weigh, expects benchmark 10-year bond to test 98.00 within session. India government seeks parliamentary nod to spend INR198.12 billion more this fiscal year beginning April. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR may rise if Wednesday's meeting of euro-zone financial heads able to come up with positive discussions on how to deal with recently-resurfaced European sovereign concerns, says Citibank chief Japan strategist Osamu Takashima. Adds, current round of debt concerns not as problematic as previous one which stemmed from Greek bond crisis as TED spreads not widening. TED spread is difference between 3-month U.S. Treasury bill and 3-month Libor; in times of financial crisis, spread widens with safe-haven U.S. Tsy getting bought whereas Libor rises. With no crisis apparent, there'll be no risk aversion and hence no EUR selling trend. EUR/USD last at 1.3680, EUR/JPY last at 113.41.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 11-15-10 0219ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101115e6bf00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
