e liquidity-driven rally will likely carry on in the next six months. The combination of inflation, rate hikes, and yuan appreciation resembles that of early 2007, except that growth now is weaker and liquidity richer," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in a research note. Among actives, ICBC (601398.SH) +6.8% at CNY5.00, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) +0.2% at CNY12.95. Shenzhen Composite Index +2.4% at 1327.99. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI has already made it clear it wants to pause policy tightening for next few months, and October headline print is probably not enough to prompt re-assessment of "wait and see" approach, says Brian Jackson at \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 . Adds, it's clear inflation remains uncomfortably high despite the rate hikes already delivered, house continues to think RBI will have more work to do in 2011, forecasts repo rate to be increased another 75 bps. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1500.98 in afternoon trade after two consecutive sessions of declines. Dealer says, "the overall sentiment remains cautious. With the focus on European sovereign debt concerns, investors aren't willing to take aggressive positions." Adds, some trading in specific stocks, but investors largely staying on sidelines. Tips range-bound trade to continue rest of session, with 1495 support, 1513 (10-day moving average) resistance. Currently 393 losers vs 304 gainers. Genting (3182.KU) +1.8% at MYR10.34 on one-off gain; Tan Chong (4405.KU) +0.9% at MYR5.44 on vehicle manufacturing license in Sabah. Pacific & Orient (6009.KU) down 10.3% at MYR0.915 after talks to sell stake in non-life insurance unit Pacific & \uc2 Orient Insurance\uc2  to U.K. insurer \uc2 Prudential PLC\uc2  (PRU.LN) terminate. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwanese vice premier Sean Chen last week demanded Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  change way it manipulates TWD. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys massive quantities of USD from state-owned banks during last 15 minutes of onshore trading session. Makes only one or two trades, but large enough to bring value of TWD vs USD back down to level near previous day's close. So, should investors sell USD during trading hours? Or wait until onshore market closes to get better price? Problem is, investors literally can't sell USD at those "closing" prices. Banks in on the trade with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't pass the prices through to market because market expects TWD will continue to appreciate. Opening price for TWD vs USD on following day invariably lower than it would have been had \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the next day, but is higher than negotiated trading price. See =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Taiwan's Blunt \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 24,173.35, off 0.7% fall midday, after Shanghai Composite index reverses earlier losses, (last +0.2%), likely due to bargain-hunting interest after recent falls, says Castor Pang, research director at Cinda; tips HSI in 23,800-24,500 range near-term. "Some investors are hunting for bargains after recent falls, and I believe trading will remain volatile in the near-term." Adds, ample liquidity also helping limit further losses in local bourse. Suggests investors avoid China properties on rate hike expectations in China. Among China properties, China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 2.8% at HK$16, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 1.3% at HK$15.18. Volume lower vs recent average at HK$62.05 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.2% at 3246.58, off intraday 3230 low, which unlikely to be tested again today, with market breadth improving tad to 2.3 decliners per gainer vs almost 3 to 1 at break; 1.11 billion shares traded worth S$1.04 billion. Index likely to remain underwater rest of session as HK, China markets also consolidating with negative bias. "We're still looking for direction. There's plenty of data coming up which may give us a bit more clarity and direction, but this is a holiday-shortened week, so no one will be out there doing too much," says economist at local brokerage. Adds, it's only natural investors stepping back after earnings season, STI's rise to 34-month highs. Singapore markets closed Wednesday for holiday. REITs broadly lower: Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) off 1.4% at S$2.06, CCT (C61U.SG) down 2.0% at S$1.45. Rig builders buck trend; Keppel (BN4.SG) +0.2% at S$10.54, SembMarine (S51.SG) +0.6% at S$4.95. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip more on larger than expected supplementary government spending plan, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.19 vs 98.24 before news, 98.15 last close. "The market was expecting extra spending but this is probably a bit more than most would have expected," says trader. Says bonds likely to slip more as sticky prices, concerns on government's fiscal profligacy continue to weigh, expects benchmark 10-year bond to test 98.00 within session. India government seeks parliamentary nod to spend INR198.12 billion more this fiscal year beginning April. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR may rise if Wednesday's meeting of euro-zone financial heads able to come up with positive discussions on how to deal with recently-resurfaced European sovereign concerns, says Citibank chief Japan strategist Osamu Takashima. Adds, current round of debt concerns not as problematic as previous one which stemmed from Greek bond crisis as TED spreads not widening. TED spread is difference between 3-month U.S. Treasury bill and 3-month Libor; in times of financial crisis, spread widens with safe-haven U.S. Tsy getting bought whereas Libor rises. With no crisis apparent, there'll be no risk aversion and hence no EUR selling trend. EUR/USD last at 1.3680, EUR/JPY last at 113.41.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  raises HK 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.8% vs 6.0% on strong consumption, supported by buoyant asset markets amid QE2. Notes HK government Friday also raised 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.5% vs 5.0%-6.0% after stronger-than-expected 3Q GDP data. "We maintain our cautiously optimistic outlook for the Hong Kong economy, as we believe that it is strategically positioned to secure its sustainable role as a service center for China and the region," report says. "Nevertheless, there is considerable concern that the sharp ascent in asset prices in the past few months and possibly in the next few months could meet with great volatility and painful corrections when capital flows reverse." (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian bond prices continue sliding as foreign investors trim holdings on resurfacing risk aversion, dealers say. Concerns on European sovereign debt prompted selloff on Indonesian bonds, says Mandiri Sekuritas's analyst Handy Yunianto; five-year yields up at 6.31% vs 6.30% late Friday; 10-year up at 7.85% vs 7.60%; 15-year up at 8.80% vs 8.74%; 20-year up at 9.09% vs 8.98%; and 30-year up at 9.15% vs 9.06%; dealers say profit-taking in bonds may continue in coming days after yields recently had fallen to fresh record lows. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 11-15-10 0216ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101115e6bf0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
