 trend; Keppel (BN4.SG) +0.2% at S$10.54, SembMarine (S51.SG) +0.6% at S$4.95. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip more on larger than expected supplementary government spending plan, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.19 vs 98.24 before news, 98.15 last close. "The market was expecting extra spending but this is probably a bit more than most would have expected," says trader. Says bonds likely to slip more as sticky prices, concerns on government's fiscal profligacy continue to weigh, expects benchmark 10-year bond to test 98.00 within session. India government seeks parliamentary nod to spend INR198.12 billion more this fiscal year beginning April. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR may rise if Wednesday's meeting of euro-zone financial heads able to come up with positive discussions on how to deal with recently-resurfaced European sovereign concerns, says Citibank chief Japan strategist Osamu Takashima. Adds, current round of debt concerns not as problematic as previous one which stemmed from Greek bond crisis as TED spreads not widening. TED spread is difference between 3-month U.S. Treasury bill and 3-month Libor; in times of financial crisis, spread widens with safe-haven U.S. Tsy getting bought whereas Libor rises. With no crisis apparent, there'll be no risk aversion and hence no EUR selling trend. EUR/USD last at 1.3680, EUR/JPY last at 113.41.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  raises HK 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.8% vs 6.0% on strong consumption, supported by buoyant asset markets amid QE2. Notes HK government Friday also raised 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.5% vs 5.0%-6.0% after stronger-than-expected 3Q GDP data. "We maintain our cautiously optimistic outlook for the Hong Kong economy, as we believe that it is strategically positioned to secure its sustainable role as a service center for China and the region," report says. "Nevertheless, there is considerable concern that the sharp ascent in asset prices in the past few months and possibly in the next few months could meet with great volatility and painful corrections when capital flows reverse." (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian bond prices continue sliding as foreign investors trim holdings on resurfacing risk aversion, dealers say. Concerns on European sovereign debt prompted selloff on Indonesian bonds, says Mandiri Sekuritas's analyst Handy Yunianto; five-year yields up at 6.31% vs 6.30% late Friday; 10-year up at 7.85% vs 7.60%; 15-year up at 8.80% vs 8.74%; 20-year up at 9.09% vs 8.98%; and 30-year up at 9.15% vs 9.06%; dealers say profit-taking in bonds may continue in coming days after yields recently had fallen to fresh record lows. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not raise key interest rates as price pressures, while still at elevated levels, may be tapering off as previous rate hikes take effect with a lag, says Anubhuti Sahay, economist at Standard Chartered bank. "Our sense is that with the October inflation number not surprising on the upside and with expectations that price pressures will cool off in coming months, the case for further monetary tightening by RBI looks pretty weak," Sahay says. Adds, revision in August inflation print to 8.82% vs 8.51% provisionally was "modest" compared with previous months' revisions of 100 bps or more. Says, RBI can be expected to tighten rates once before end-March if inflation surprises on upside by wide margin. India October WPI at 8.58% vs 8.50% in Dow Jones poll. RBI's next rate-setting meeting on Dec. 16. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 11-15-10 0215ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101115e6bf0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\pics