\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan's Blunt  \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2 -WSJ BLOG\b0\par\par\uc2 1,531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2010\par 07:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwanese vice premier Sean Chen last week demanded Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  change way it manipulates TWD. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys massive quantities of USD from state-owned banks during last 15 minutes of onshore trading session. Makes only one or two trades, but large enough to bring value of TWD vs USD back down to level near previous day's close. So, should investors sell USD during trading hours? Or wait until onshore market closes to get better price? Problem is, investors literally can't sell USD at those "closing" prices. Banks in on the trade with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't pass the prices through to market because market expects TWD will continue to appreciate. Opening price for TWD vs USD on following day invariably lower than it would have been had \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the next day, but is higher than negotiated trading price. See =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Taiwan's Blunt \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 24,173.35, off 0.7% fall midday, after Shanghai Composite index reverses earlier losses, (last +0.2%), likely due to bargain-hunting interest after recent falls, says Castor Pang, research director at Cinda; tips HSI in 23,800-24,500 range near-term. "Some investors are hunting for bargains after recent falls, and I believe trading will remain volatile in the near-term." Adds, ample liquidity also helping limit further losses in local bourse. Suggests investors avoid China properties on rate hike expectations in China. Among China properties, China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 2.8% at HK$16, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 1.3% at HK$15.18. Volume lower vs recent average at HK$62.05 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.2% at 3246.58, off intraday 3230 low, which unlikely to be tested again today, with market breadth improving tad to 2.3 decliners per gainer vs almost 3 to 1 at break; 1.11 billion shares traded worth S$1.04 billion. Index likely to remain underwater rest of session as HK, China markets also consolidating with negative bias. "We're still looking for direction. There's plenty of data coming up which may give us a bit more clarity and direction, but this is a holiday-shortened week, so no one will be out there doing too much," says economist at local brokerage. Adds, it's only natural investors stepping back after earnings season, STI's rise to 34-month highs. Singapore markets closed Wednesday for holiday. REITs broadly lower: Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) off 1.4% at S$2.06, CCT (C61U.SG) down 2.0% at S$1.45. Rig builders buck trend; Keppel (BN4.SG) +0.2% at S$10.54, SembMarine (S51.SG) +0.6% at S$4.95. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip more on larger than expected supplementary government spending plan, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.19 vs 98.24 before news, 98.15 last close. "The market was expecting extra spending but this is probably a bit more than most would have expected," says trader. Says bonds likely to slip more as sticky prices, concerns on government's fiscal profligacy continue to weigh, expects benchmark 10-year bond to test 98.00 within session. India government seeks parliamentary nod to spend INR198.12 billion more this fiscal year beginning April. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR may rise if Wednesday's meeting of euro-zone financial heads able to come up with positive discussions on how to deal with recently-resurfaced European sovereign concerns, says Citibank chief Japan strategist Osamu Takashima. Adds, current round of debt concerns not as problematic as previous one which stemmed from Greek bond crisis as TED spreads not widening. TED spread is difference between 3-month U.S. Treasury bill and 3-month Libor; in times of financial crisis, spread widens with safe-haven U.S. Tsy getting bought whereas Libor rises. With no crisis apparent, there'll be no risk aversion and hence no EUR selling trend. EUR/USD last at 1.3680, EUR/JPY last at 113.41.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  raises HK 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.8% vs 6.0% on strong consumption, supported by buoyant asset markets amid QE2. Notes HK government Friday also raised 2010 GDP growth forecast to 6.5% vs 5.0%-6.0% after stronger-than-expected 3Q GDP data. "We maintain our cautiously optimistic outlook for the Hong Kong economy, as we believe that it is strategically positioned to secure its sustainable role as a service center for China and the region," report says. "Nevertheless, there is considerable concern that the sharp ascent in asset prices in the past few months and possibly in the next few months could meet with great volatility and painful corrections when capital flows reverse." (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian bond prices continue sliding as foreign investors trim holdings on resurfacing risk aversion, dealers say. Concerns on European sovereign debt prompted selloff on Indonesian bonds, says Mandiri Sekuritas's analyst Handy Yunianto; five-year yields up at 6.31% vs 6.30% late Friday; 10-year up at 7.85% vs 7.60%; 15-year up at 8.80% vs 8.74%; 20-year up at 9.09% vs 8.98%; and 30-year up at 9.15% vs 9.06%; dealers say profit-taking in bonds may continue in coming days after yields recently had fallen to fresh record lows. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not raise key interest rates as price pressures, while still at elevated levels, may be tapering off as previous rate hikes take effect with a lag, says Anubhuti Sahay, economist at Standard Chartered bank. "Our sense is that with the October inflation number not surprising on the upside and with expectations that price pressures will cool off in coming months, the case for further monetary tightening by RBI looks pretty weak," Sahay says. Adds, revision in August inflation print to 8.82% vs 8.51% provisionally was "modest" compared with previous months' revisions of 100 bps or more. Says, RBI can be expected to tighten rates once before end-March if inflation surprises on upside by wide margin. India October WPI at 8.58% vs 8.50% in Dow Jones poll. RBI's next rate-setting meeting on Dec. 16. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI may extend pause on rates till next 3 to 4 quarters on easing of pace of primary articles, says Yes Bank's chief economist, Shubhada Rao. India October inflation rate rose 8.58% on-year vs 8.50% in Dow Jones Newswires poll of 15 economists. Primary articles rose 16.68% on-year, food articles rose 14.13%. "Food prices are likely to fall over next few months while commodity prices may see a measured rise," Rao says. Expects headline inflation to continue on downward trajectory; adds, likely to touch "comfortable" levels by December-January period. (suhita.poddar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD up on stronger USD offshore, weaker local stocks, says trader at U.K. bank. Tips pair in 7.7515-7.7540 band near-term. "The rebound of the U.S. dollar against the euro prompted some investment banks to buy back the U.S. dollar on short-covering, pushing up the pair," trader says. HSI down 0.4%. Pair last at 7.7526 vs 7.7517 this morning, compared with 7.7522 Friday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY touches 82.98, highest since Oct. 7, as short-term investors cover USD-short positions, spurred by higher U.S. Treasury yields, strong Japanese shares, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. Though selling from Japanese exporters around last level of 82.95 heavy, he adds "it's just a matter of time" before pair rises above 83.00, where many stop-loss buying orders placed. Tips resistance 84.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR rises further to 3.1310 vs 3.1090 late Friday, on worries over Ireland's debt problems, rising U.S. Treasury yields, says dealer with local bank. "The pair is tracking other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (against the U.S. dollar)," says dealer. RHB Research says in line with previous week's "hammer-like" candle, USD/MYR recorded bullish candle after rebounding from weekly low of 3.066 last week. "Chartwise, as both the momentum indicators have also ticked higher, the pair is likely to continue moving up this week, in our view," says RHB; tips resistance at 3.1600 near term. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 11-15-10 0158ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101115e6bf0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
