\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/ \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Taiwan's Blunt  \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2010\par 06:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's China Real Time Report blog at blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime.)\par \par \uc2 Of all the roles central banks are asked to perform, keeping a lid on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation by directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is perhaps one of the trickiest. And it's certainly not made any easier when politicians weigh in with their two cents on how it should be done.\par \par \uc2 In Taiwan's case, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of China may do well to listen to the pols.\par \par \uc2 In a rare display of government criticism, Taiwanese vice premier Sean Chen delivered a broadside against the CBC in the Legislature on Wednesday, demanding the bank radically change the way it manipulates the New Taiwan Dollar.\par \par \uc2 The issue isn't over whether the huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's been underway in recent months to curb the NTD's unstoppable gains is in fact money well spent. This is Asia after all, and governments across the region are actively resisting pressure to appreciate. Taiwan in particular has a reputation for being one of the most -- if not the most \u226\'00\'E2 " \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  of central banks in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Rather, it's a question of whether they can get more bang from their buck.\par \par \uc2 The way the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  is quite unique. It buys dollars in massive quantities from state-owned banks during the last 15 minutes of the onshore five-hour trading session. It typically makes only one or two trades, but their size is large enough to bring the value of the NTD against the U.S. dollar back down to a level somewhere very near to the previous day's close.\par \par \uc2 So, should investors sell the U.S. dollar during trading hours? Or to wait until the onshore market closes so that they can get a better price?\par \par \uc2 The tricky part is that investors literally can't sell the U.S. dollar at those "closing" prices. The banks that are in on the trade with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't pass the prices through to the market because market expectations \u226\'00\'E2 " as reflected in the offshore nondeliverable forwards market \u226\'00\'E2 " are that the NTD will continue appreciate. The opening price for the NTD against the dollar the following day is invariably lower than it would have been had the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the next day, but is higher than the negotiated trading price. That dilutes the benefit to exporters \u226\'00\'E2 " for whom the government tries to control appreciation in the first place - who have to settle their trades at less advantageous level than where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In short, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high jinks have managed to slow the headline rate of appreciation, but the closing prices are in effect fictional.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that the Taiwanese style of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively resets the closing level each day, but fails to send a clear message to the market about where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be. In other words, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has failed to draw a line in the sand and instead the market has come to expect that the gradual appreciation of the NTD is the norm.\par \par \uc2 The contrast with Japan and Singapore \u226\'00\'E2 " neither of whom shy from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u226\'00\'E2 " is instructive. They have internal moving target levels at which they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. They don't broadcast those levels in advance, but by being willing to trade to defend those levels they signal to the market how much movement they're willing to accept.\par \par \uc2 And by knowing the boundaries the market can trade accordingly--a superior approach to having the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  press reset each day.\par \par \uc2 One might argue Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have worked to a certain extent, given the Taiwan dollar has risen less than its rival Korean won. But it's a case of apples and oranges: Korea's local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds are offering more decent returns than their Taiwanese counterparts, attracting additional capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime is best judged on its own terms \u226\'00\'E2 " and surely there has to be a better way.\par \par \uc2 --Aries Poon\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 15-11-10 0517GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101115e6bf000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
