\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2010\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN SEES G-20 LEADERS AGREEING ON NEED FOR CURR ACCT GUIDELINES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister said Tuesday that the leaders of the Group of 20 industrial and developing countries may agree on the need for "indicative guidelines" to mend global trade imbalances, but they probably won't settle the specific details, and instead, will leave that to the finance ministers. (published 0118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  REGULATOR ISSUES RULES TO CURB HOT MONEY INFLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator on Tuesday announced a series of measures to further crack down on hot money inflows. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SEPT UNADJUSTED CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS RISES 24.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's current account surplus expanded in September as imports increased at a slower pace, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday. (published 0000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA OCTOBER PRODUCER PRICE GROWTH SLOWS; +0.1% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's producer prices slowed considerably in October from the previous month as prices of fresh food stabilized, giving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  room to maneuver in its efforts to control rising inflationary pressures. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA FINANCE MINISTER: SOME MAJOR \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ISSUERS HAVE OVERLY LOOSE MONETARY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Finance Minister Xie Xuren said Tuesday that some issuers of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have had excessively loose monetary policies, an apparent reference to the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy of quantitative easing that has prompted a series of complaints from Chinese officials. (published 0122 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECONOMIST: U.S. POLICY MEANS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RISE PRESSURE FOR SOME COUNTRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's policy of quantitative easing will lead to hot money inflows and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation pressures for some countries, a former Chinese lawmaker and prominent economist said at a forum on Tuesday. (published 0236 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA VICE PREMIER: FINANCIAL VOLATILITY AFFECTING MARKET CONFIDENCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Vice Premier Wang Qishan said Tuesday he sees excessive global liquidity, adding that volatility in international financial markets is affecting market confidence. (published 0146 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US'S SUMMERS: EXPECT 'SATISFACTORY' OUTCOME TO RESOLVE GLOBAL IMBALANCES AT G-20 SUMMIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The leaders of the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations will likely come to a satisfactory agreement to resolve global imbalances when they meet in Seoul later this week, Director of the White House National Economic Council Larry Summers said Tuesday. (published 0237 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OECD: MAJOR ASEAN ECONOMIES RETURNING TO PRE-CRISIS GROWTH RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The six major economies of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations are seeing strong growth and should be able to post 2011-15 annual GDP expansion of 6.0% on average, equal to what they saw before the 2008 global financial crisis, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said in a report Tuesday. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA VOWS A$3.45B IN CUTS TO KEEP SURPLUS PROMISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government is on track to restore its budget to a modest surplus in three years, well ahead of many of its developed-country peers. (published 0249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN BUSINESS CONFIDENCE FALLS 2 PTS TO +8 IN OCT VS SEPT - NAB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business confidence fell for a second straight month in October, hurt by the prospect of rising interest rates and a surging Australian dollar's impact on exporters. (published 0030 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN, US TO LAUNCH DIALOGUE TO SECURE RARE EARTH MINERALS - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo and Washington have decided to launch high-level talks on resources and energy in a bid to secure rare earth minerals amid stalled exports of them from China, Kyodo News reported Tuesday citing sources close to Japan-U.S. relations. (published 0136 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO ALLOW LOCAL GOVERNMENTS TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN PROPERTY SECTOR - REPORT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is planning measures that will allow local governments to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the property market to prevent fast increases in property prices, the state-run Securities Times reported Tuesday, citing unnamed sources. (published 0043 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RATE HIKES POSE BIGGEST RISK TO CHINA MORTGAGE LOANS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any further interest-rate hikes in China would be the biggest risk for domestic mortgage loans totaling trillions of yuan, the state-run China Securities Journal reported Tuesday, citing a government think tank. (published 0114 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TO INVEST $6.6B IN INDONESIAN INFRASTRUCTURE - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has pledged to invest $6.6 billion in infrastructure projects in Indonesia, the Jakarta Globe reported Tuesday. (published 0104 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF: CHINA SELLS NET Y769.2B JAPAN FINANCIAL ASSETS IN SEP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's sales of Japanese financial assets including government bonds declined in September, data from Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Tuesday. (published 0126 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OVERSEA-CHINESE BANKING PRICES US$500 MLN TIER 2 SUBORDINATED NOTES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Tuesday said it has priced US$500 million of subordinated notes at 99.367% and expects to issue the notes on Nov. 15. (published 2341 GMT) [ 09-11-10 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101109e6b90007z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
