\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(10/22) IMF Calls For Higher Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2010\par 20:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 5\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Gaurav Raghuvanshi, P.r. Venkat  and Costas Paris \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE -- Asian countries should allow their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rise and withdraw stimulus measures as they grapple with surging capital inflows that threaten to fuel inflation, the International Monetary Fund said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Asia, including Japan, is likely to grow 8% this year, faster than the 7% estimated in April, but the region's economies need to rebalance their growth toward stronger domestic demand, as imports of goods and services by developed nations aren't likely to return to precrisis levels anytime soon, the IMF said in its latest Regional Economic Outlook for the Asia-Pacific.\par \par \uc2 "In view of the strong economic expansion that is under way, and emerging signs of inflationary pressure in some economies, Asia has reached the threshold to normalize policy stances across the region," the IMF said in the report. "Greater exchange-rate flexibility will be an important component of policy tightening."\par \par \uc2 Asian nations have been rushing to deal with rapid capital flows as investors shift away from the economic uncertainties of the U.S. and Europe. The shift has shaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and prompted several Asian nations to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising in value. Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which bears the brunt of global scrutiny over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls -- has let the renminbi rise but still faces pressure to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float more freely. The tensions will likely dominate the Group of 20 talks taking place in South Korea this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Asian countries have comprehensive tools to tackle capital inflows, Anoop Singh, director of the IMF's Asia and Pacific Department, said at a press conference in Jakarta.\par \par \uc2 "The challenge is to channel these inflows beyond the financial market, such as to infrastructure projects," he said.\par \par \uc2 The capital inflows haven't gone beyond their peak levels before the 2008 financial crisis and the IMF doesn't expect governments to erect capital controls, Mr. Singh said.\par \par \uc2 Still, further tightening of monetary conditions in Asia may be needed, including increased exchange-rate appreciation, the IMF said in the report.\par \par \uc2 A faster withdrawal of fiscal stimulus would also help guard against the risks of overheating and a buildup of financial imbalances, the report said.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the recent tightening, financial conditions generally remain accommodative in many emerging Asian economies compared with before the crisis (especially in China, the Philippines, and Thailand), as the policy easing of 2009 has not been completely unwound, equity valuations remain elevated, and bank credit continues to recover," the report said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said managing capital inflows is another major policy challenge for Asia, as U.S. monetary conditions are likely to remain supportive for an extended period and global interest rates may stay low.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar, Thai baht, Indian rupee and Singapore dollar have racked up substantial gains in the past two months. The Chinese yuan has also appreciated but hasn't kept pace with its regional peers.\par \par \uc2 While the yuan has risen against the dollar since mid-June, when Beijing said it would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move more freely, it remains roughly at its level of the late 1990s in real effective terms, as of early October, the IMF said. The yuan has risen 2.7% against the U.S. dollar so far this year, far less than a 11.7% rise for the Thai baht and an 8.0% rise for the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said the yuan "remains substantially below the level consistent with medium-term fundamentals," citing China's rapid pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve accumulation, its large trade surplus and its productivity.\par \par \uc2 The IMF report pointed to a possible property bubble in some of China's larger cities, though it said that for the country as a whole, property prices don't seem to be significantly above fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Also Thursday, the IMF reiterated that it supports China's move this week to boost interest rates for the first time in nearly three years. "Given the pace of recovery in China, it is appropriate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue to withdraw monetary stimulus and return the pace of credit growth to more normal levels," an IMF spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Andreas Ismar in Jakarta contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2010 14:21 ET (18:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101021e6al005ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
