\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: Shock China Rate Hike Rattles  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 14:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Rewrites, adds comments, detail.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Katie Martin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--News that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to raise interest rates sent shockwaves through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Tuesday as it ramped up speculation that global political powers could be nearing a deal on exchange-rate tensions.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar shot higher while other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the Australian dollar, euro and yen, all weakened in reaction to the news that the People's Bank of China unexpectedly raised the one-year lending and deposit rates by 25 basis points each, effective Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With finance ministers from the Group of 20 leading industrialized and developing economies due to meet at the end of this week, the move has raised the prospect that China is set to allow a faster climb in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "The timing is significant," said Neil Mellor, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London. "China knows that the market will look at this and put two and two together and assume there's going to be a deal."\par \par \uc2 The surprise PBOC decision will likely defuse broadening U.S. complaints that China's monetary policies have been focused on keeping down the yuan to make its exports cheaper in western markets.\par \par \uc2 Friday, the U.S. Treasury Department delayed a report that may have labelled China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. Political pressure within the U.S. to take action on Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness remains persistent, however.\par \par \uc2 Tensions in the $4 trillion-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are not limited to strained relations between the U.S. and China. As the dollar has tumbled in recent weeks amid expectations for further monetary easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve, a huge range of other countries have tried to bat their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down in an effort to protect exporters, in a trend dubbed a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war. This week's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of 20 nations, and next month's G-20 leaders' summit, are seen as key forums to find a solution.\par \par \uc2 If China were to let the yuan climb faster, other Asian countries would be likely to do the same, as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's competitive advantage would lessen. That would mean less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Asian authorities to weaken their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would in turn indirectly lead to a stronger dollar. Asian authorities would likely push for the U.S. to support the dollar itself in return for a pledge to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  climb.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner hinted at greater support for the dollar late Monday, when he said that the U.S. wouldn't devalue the greenback. "It's not viable and we will not engage in it," he said.\par \par \uc2 So far, the biggest reaction to the PBOC's step in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  space is in the Australian dollar, which has tumbled by over 1%--a hefty intraday move--to hit its lowest point in a week at US$0.9777. That reflects the large scale of Australia's commodities exports to China, which could stumble if the PBOC rate rise generates a slowdown in the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 The euro has also dropped by 0.5% to $1.3862 against the dollar, while the dollar has leapt by 0.4% against the yen to the day's high of Y81.70.\par \par \uc2 The broad rise in the U.S. dollar indicates that investor confidence in the global economic recovery has been shaken, pushing funds into the greenback, which is traditionally seen as relatively safe.\par \par \uc2 "The policy hike damaged risk sentiment somewhat," said Geoffrey Yu, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2010 08:38 ET (12:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101019e6aj002wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
