\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: World Bank Sees Yuan Rise As Way To Stem China Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 05:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from World Bank official, background on Chinese consumer price index, recent comment from Premier Wen Jiabao, World Bank GDP growth estimates for East Asian developing countries.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Andrew Monahan \par   Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--China could help stem the rising inflation it is likely to face in the short term by guiding the yuan higher, the World Bank said in a report issued Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 While further asset price increases would pose a key risk to China's rapidly growing economy, "a gradual rise" in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nominal exchange rate could "help stem price pressures," the Washington-based international financial institution said.\par \par \uc2 A good example of "gradual appreciation" would be the roughly 20% rise in the yuan that China let occur from July 2005 to July 2008, said Vikram Nehru, chief economist for East Asia and Pacific Region of the World Bank. "It was done gradually, and I think that kind of pace was something that the Chinese economy was able to absorb fairly easily," Nehru told reporters at a briefing in Tokyo on the report.\par \par \uc2 While any price increases will likely "be contained," they would stem in part "from international prices for industrial raw materials," the World Bank said. A stronger yuan would lower import costs, helping to offset any such price rises.\par \par \uc2 The report comes as China faces mounting pressure from major trading partners such as the U.S. to steer the yuan higher. Tensions have been rising over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues ahead of meetings of finance ministers from the Group of 20 advanced and developing economies in South Korea later this week.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen "more modestly," the World Bank said, than those of other countries covered in its East Asia and Pacific Economic Update 2010, Volume 2. The yuan has risen around 8% in real effective terms from before the global financial crisis that started in 2008, while such exchange rates in the other countries in the region are 10-15% higher.\par \par \uc2 Since China decided to let the yuan float more freely in June, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 2.4% against the dollar in nominal terms. In real terms, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen by over 4% since January, but higher inflation "has dominated the appreciation so far," the World Bank said. Real exchange rates reflect nominal exchange rates adjusted for inflation.\par \par \uc2 The World Bank's report comes after People's Bank of China Deputy Gov. Yi Gang said Monday that yuan reform is good for policy effectiveness and easing inflation. China's consumer price index rose 3.5% in August from a year earlier, up from July's 3.3% increase. Yi said, however, that despite the benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform helping prevent sharper price rises, change has to be gradual.\par \par \uc2 China's top leadership has consistently stressed that it has no intention to appreciate the yuan more rapidly. Premier Wen Jiabao told European Union officials earlier this month that Beijing would stick to its gradual approach. "If we increase the yuan by 20% or 40%, as some people are calling for, many of our factories will shut down and society will be in turmoil," he said.\par \par \uc2 Continued modest yuan appreciation will likely fail to silence critics of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the U.S. and other governments. While the U.S. Treasury Department put off a decision Friday on whether to label China a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" until after the G-20 leaders summit next month in South Korea, the issue is expected to continue coloring international discussions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to the small rises in the yuan, the larger rises in exchange rates in the region have come as a "surge in capital inflows caused exchange rates to appreciate strongly," the World Bank said. Surging investment in the region has come as substantial monetary easing in the U.S. has led "to a very large, sharp increase in global liquidity over a very short period of time," said the World Bank's Nehru.\par \par \uc2 Global investors have piled into developing economies in East Asia as they have rebounded vigorously from the global financial crisis. The World Bank raised its estimate for real gross domestic product growth for the countries to 8.9% in 2010, up 0.2 percentage points from its previous forecast, led by an estimated 9.5% growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.\par \par \uc2 Some central banks in East Asia have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have hurt their country's export-dependent economies.\par \par \uc2 But such moves have had only limited success. They could even fan inflationary flames, if they result in more money in the countries' financial systems, the World Bank said.\par \par \uc2 "In the absence of appropriate sterilization this could add to inflationary pressures" in the region, it said. Sterilization refers to the process by which a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mops up the extra liquidity injected into the economy through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Joy C. Shaw and Esther Fung contributed to this article.) [ 19-10-10 0358GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101019e6aj0006y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
