\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian Summary\b0\par\par\uc2 1,795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets higher; Nikkei +1.9%, S&P/ASX +1.7%, Shanghai Composite +1.0%, HSI +1.1%, STI +0.1%, KLCI flat, NZX-50 +1.0%, Kospi +1.0%, Taiex +1.3%, SET +0.5%, Sensex down 0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD weaker vs majors, Asian units, hit by continued weakness ahead of expected easing by Fed, and quickly accelerated by surprise MAS decision to tighten monetary policy. USD/JPY last at 81.21 vs 81.74 late NY trade Monday, EUR/USD at 1.4069 vs 1.3964, EUR/JPY at 114.27 vs 114.14. USD/CAD touches parity, last at 1.0005. USD/CNY hit fresh intraday low under current system at 6.6560 vs 6.6641 late Wednesday in Asia; central parity also set at fresh record low of 6.6582 vs 6.6693 yesterday. MAS says it will "slightly" increase slope of trading band of SGD--its main policy lever--against basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; adds, will also widen policy band "slightly." It had been widely expected to keep policy unchanged; USD/SGD hit all-time low of 1.2886 vs 1.3050 late Asia yesterday. Island state's GDP contracted 19.8%, on-quarter, seasonally-adjusted, annualized vs 17.5% decline expected, +27.3% in 2Q. On-year, GDP +10.3% vs +19.6% in 2Q. BOK keeps benchmark interest rate steady at 2.25%, pausing in tightening cycle for 3rd straight month amid increased uncertainty about recovery in global economy, rising tensions over \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates; governor Kim Choong-soo says it's not appropriate for a country to comment on another's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. Australian consumer inflationary expectations sharply up in October; Melbourne Institute Survey of Consumer Inflationary Expectations median expected inflation rate rose to 3.8% from 3.1% in September. NZ August seasonally adjusted retail sales flat on-month vs +0.4% expected. Gold, silver extend gains to hit new highs; spot gold up $8.10 from New York close at $1,380.00/oz after hitting new record at $1,380.32 earlier. Spot silver at $24.29/oz, just off 30-year high at $24.37. November Nymex crude oil futures +70 cents at $84.44/bbl on Globex. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume about same as yesterday's half-day at HK$3.586 billion, but as percentage of total market volume slips to 4.6% vs 6.5% at Wednesday's half-day, suggesting bears remain wary of market strength, with HSI rising for 8th session out of 9. China Life (2628.HK) leads with HK$596.49 million, followed by A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$332.56 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$264.98 million. \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK), HKEx (0388.HK) round out top 5, with HK$209.91 million, HK$209.70 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds down, as funds switch to stock market for higher returns on back of stronger risk appetite, local trader says; but any drastic gains in yields unlikely, as local bonds likely to be supported by inflows. "Foreign money usually enters the bond market here first, which makes it popular among investors despite very low yields." Yields on 10-year government bond end at 1.1890% vs 1.1808% yesterday, 5-year yield at 0.8465% vs 0.8430%. Tips 10-year paper to trade in 1.1870%-1.1910% band tomorrow. Taiex ends +1.3% at 8215.45. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are up 0.20 at 131.77 but given the recent trading pattern, the contract is expected to remain sideways. \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  tips resistance at 132.20 and 132.89, while support is at 131.13 and 130.63. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end higher due to gains in heavyweight TSMC (2330.TW), banks, petrochemical stocks; Taiex +1.3% at 8215.45 in moderate volume at NT$120.89 billion. Capital Securities analyst Diana Wu tips Taiex tomorrow to test 8252 immediate resistance, then 8310; "but the key thing to watch is (daily) trading volume. If it can rise to NT$140 billion or NT$150 billion, upward momentum will be formed, otherwise it will remain in consolidation mode." Adds, strong TWD lingering overhang on electronics stocks going forward; TSMC ends +1.3% at NT$61.80, UMC (2303.TW) +1.5% at NT$13.70. Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) +5.5% at NT$82.00, after Bank of America-Merrill Lynch upgrades to Buy vs Underperform on higher product margins; Nan Ya (1303.TW) also +4.4% at NT$66.70. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR extends losses, touches fresh intraday low of 44.09 on heavy selling pressure ahead of \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 's share sale Monday, says trader with state-run bank. Pair now at 44.20 vs 44.52 late Wednesday. "Inflows are particularly heavy today, largely driven by foreign funds' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conversion into rupees. The pair may touch the 44.00 level if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something that is being keenly watched at these levels," trader says. Adds, oil importers had been sitting on sidelines but 44.10 provided good level for buying. Tips pair in 44.00-44.30 band for session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Korea likely to raise rates by 25 bps by year-end, given BOK's fairly positive economic assessment, inflation risks stemming from higher commodity prices, rising domestic demand, according to \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Song-yi Kim; notes, "it is clear enough that the BOK's decision to keep its policy rate unchanged today is dominated by its concern about Korea's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...but we believe that there is still some chance that the BOK will have to move on rates before the year-end...the MPC still remains biased toward further tightening." Adds, domestic consumption looks to remain supportive of overall economic growth, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength likely to boost consumption, through better purchasing power. BOK earlier today kept policy rate unchanged at 2.25% for third straight month. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.5% at 997.94 at midday break, flirting yet again with 1000 point level, in active trade as PTT (PTT.TH) group shares push higher on rising oil prices, while banking sector also bubbling along ahead of expected robust 3Q earnings. "I think the SET Index should test 1000 today for sure, but I don't think it can stay above 1000 today as it's a key resistance level for investors," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  strategist Mayuree Chowvikran. Resistance at 1000, support at 995. Among top actives, \uc2 PTT Aromatics and Refining\uc2  (PTTAR.TH) +3.5% at THB29.50, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.0% at THB312, \uc2 PTT Exploration and Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.2% at THB174, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +1.7% at THB17.70, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) +0.9% at THB161.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] KRW IRS down sharply as BOK's decision to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25% for 3rd straight month fan view \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to resume tightening until next year, participants say; 2-year IRS down sharply at 3.180% vs 3.272% late yesterday, 5-year down at 3.540% vs 3.618%, December bond futures +48 ticks at 113.23. Yield curve steepening slightly and "I think bonds could strengthen a bit more from here given that people are thin