\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/INR Extends Losses, Inflows; 44.00 Key Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 08:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR extends losses, touches fresh intraday low of 44.09 on heavy selling pressure ahead of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 's share sale Monday, says trader with state-run bank. Pair now at 44.20 vs 44.52 late Wednesday. "Inflows are particularly heavy today, largely driven by foreign funds' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conversion into rupees. The pair may touch the 44.00 level if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , something that is being keenly watched at these levels," trader says. Adds, oil importers had been sitting on sidelines but 44.10 provided good level for buying. Tips pair in 44.00-44.30 band for session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Korea likely to raise rates by 25 bps by year-end, given BOK's fairly positive economic assessment, inflation risks stemming from higher commodity prices, rising domestic demand, according to \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Song-yi Kim; notes, "it is clear enough that the BOK's decision to keep its policy rate unchanged today is dominated by its concern about Korea's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...but we believe that there is still some chance that the BOK will have to move on rates before the year-end...the MPC still remains biased toward further tightening." Adds, domestic consumption looks to remain supportive of overall economic growth, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength likely to boost consumption, through better purchasing power. BOK earlier today kept policy rate unchanged at 2.25% for third straight month. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.5% at 997.94 at midday break, flirting yet again with 1000 point level, in active trade as PTT (PTT.TH) group shares push higher on rising oil prices, while banking sector also bubbling along ahead of expected robust 3Q earnings. "I think the SET Index should test 1000 today for sure, but I don't think it can stay above 1000 today as it's a key resistance level for investors," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  strategist Mayuree Chowvikran. Resistance at 1000, support at 995. Among top actives, \uc2 PTT Aromatics and Refining\uc2  (PTTAR.TH) +3.5% at THB29.50, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.0% at THB312, \uc2 PTT Exploration and Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.2% at THB174, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +1.7% at THB17.70, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) +0.9% at THB161.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] KRW IRS down sharply as BOK's decision to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25% for 3rd straight month fan view \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to resume tightening until next year, participants say; 2-year IRS down sharply at 3.180% vs 3.272% late yesterday, 5-year down at 3.540% vs 3.618%, December bond futures +48 ticks at 113.23. Yield curve steepening slightly and "I think bonds could strengthen a bit more from here given that people are thinking no rate hike will be in the pipeline for the rest of this year," though there's not much room for yields to fall today, says swap broker with foreign brokerage. Says 3-year swaps could slip to near 3.000% level before year-end, last down at 3.335% vs 3.422%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,379.60/oz, up $7.70 after touching new record at $1,380.32 at 0458 GMT. "It's momentum-buying at the moment. "There's no real resistance so $1,400 looks likely," says Singapore-based trader. Price mainly driven by buying on Globex, with OTC market quiet, although some scrap selling emerging, he says. Struggling dollar still primary driver with EUR/USD at 1.4083, just off intraday and 10-month high of 1.4094 hit earlier. U.S. August trade balance, September PPI, Oct 9 weekly jobless claims, all at 1230 GMT, main focus on data front later. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered raises short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on TWD to Overweight vs Neutral, boosted by expectation CNY to extend gains over upcoming quarters. "It seems reasonable that Taiwan dollar appreciation will play a role, alongside (Chinese Yuan) appreciation, in helping to trim global imbalances." House revises down 4Q USD/TWD target to 30.75 vs 31.15, 1Q USD/TWD target to 30.40 vs 31.00. Adds, TWD typically outperforms in 4Q, 1Q. USD/TWD last at 30.705, touching fresh 26-month low at 30.540 earlier. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] HK Chief Executive Donald Tsang's policy address managed to give property developers a scare, even if initiatives probably won't do much to tackle HK's sky-high property prices. Property stocks fell Wednesday during address as measures could hit margins short term, but recovered intraday as players realized measures relatively tepid, within expectations. Measures are not aggressive enough, according to Irene Chow, strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , specifically government's aim to add 35,000 flats/year. "Given that the new supply in 2009 was 22,500, which was very low, there is an intention to increase supply, but it's still not a lot." Tsang emphasized will continue to "minimize \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the property market" in keeping with HK's hands-off market policies. But difficulties facing "sandwich class" leaving government with "no alternative but to step up the pace of its direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 CB Richard Ellis\uc2 . See: =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: How Donald Tsang's Housing Policies Will Affect Developers (isabella.steger@wsj.com; alice.truong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian markets reflect an increased correlation to the U.S. markets, according to a note by Russell Global Index. During 14 years of available returns for the Russell Global Index there has been a similar correlation pattern between India and China with the U.S. broad market Russell 3000 Index, note says; adds, throughout period, Russell 3000 reflected average 2-year correlation of 0.50 with Russell India Index and 0.48 with Russell China Index. "While the Russell China Index has correlated to the U.S. market at a relatively steady degree during the 14-year period from September of 1996 through September of 2010, staying within a range of 0.30 and 0.75, India's correlation to the Russell 3000 has increased more so since 2006, reaching a 0.80 correlation point in 2009." (john.kumar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday +0.5% at 3628.477 in moderate volume, off earlier high of 3643.771, led by foreign-buying in most blue chips amid hopes for strong 9-month earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3650. "Overall sentiment however, looks cautious as local institutional funds continue to take profit," says trader at Lautandhana Securities . Foreigners net buyers of IDR180 billion worth of shares. Among gainers; gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) +3.1% at IDR4,125, coal miner Adaro (ADRO.JK) +4.8% at IDR2,200, while Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) also +2.5% at IDR4,100. Profit-taking hits \uc2 XL Axiata\uc2  (EXCL.JK) down 4.3% at IDR5,550. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] As many other developed economies consider further easing of monetary policy, Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands alone in contemplating further rate rises, says Heard on the Street; since RBA left rates on hold last week, surprising many, strong consumer sentiment and jobs data has made a hike next month seem a certainty. But AUD approaching parity with USD, a worry for Australia's exporting industries; already there's talk success of the country's mining sector, on the back of Chinese growth, is causing "Dutch disease" -- the concern being that success of resources industry could dampen prospects for rest of economy. See: HEARD ON THE STREET: Australia's Lonely \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.2% at 458.66 as foreigners extend their recent buying-streak amid growing optimism more banks will lower lending rates, while government continues to help companies lower \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs. "Market sentiment will improve because more banks are expected to cut their lending rates. Also, foreigners are buying these days so we expect more gains tomorrow," says Eurocapital Securities analyst. Volume thin with 23.3 million shares valued at VND597 billion traded. Among actives, Meco (MCG.VH) ends +4.7% at VND17,900, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Fertilizer (DPM.VH) ends +1.8% at VND33,500, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 0.3% at VND30,000. Tips key index to move in 460-465 Friday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares turn flat after breaching psychological 1,500 level; profit-taking in blue-chips erase early gains; KLCI up 0.35 points at 1,497.32 midday. Dealer says short term positive trend intact, but adds 1,500 to act as strong barrier with profit-taking likely to limit gains at any rally; tips 1,495-1,505 range for rest of session. Investors continue to favor construction stocks on hopes of positive newsflow from 2011 budget tomorrow; sub-index +0.7%; Market breadth slightly positive with 363 gainers vs 334 decliners; gainers include plantation stocks \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +3.9% at MYR19.32, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) +1.0% at MYR5.80; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +1.3% at MYR3.10; Manulife (1058.KU) +12% at MYR3.18 after major shareholder makes offer to buy out rest of shares in insurer; among decliners \uc2 GuocoLand\uc2  (1503.KU) down 5.0% at MYR1.15, MK Land (8893.KU) down 2.8% at MYR0.345, both after yesterday's gains.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0204ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
