ners; gainers include plantation stocks \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +3.9% at MYR19.32, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) +1.0% at MYR5.80; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +1.3% at MYR3.10; Manulife (1058.KU) +12% at MYR3.18 after major shareholder makes offer to buy out rest of shares in insurer; among decliners \uc2 GuocoLand\uc2  (1503.KU) down 5.0% at MYR1.15, MK Land (8893.KU) down 2.8% at MYR0.345, both after yesterday's gains.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD parity seems inevitable, but concerns growing a pullback could be in cards if central banks don't do what market expects them to, says Sydney-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist. "Fundamentally, there is little standing in the way and it's pretty much at parity already. But there remains this possibility the RBA won't go (to higher rates) in November on the threat of commercial banks raising lending rates. And the FOMC could decide to implement other measures before they buy Treasury securities outright," says the strategist. AUD/USD recently at 0.9960, just off new post-float high of 0.9982 hit in early afternoon trading. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares pare gains as investors take some money off table with prices at 29-month highs. Market breadth flat vs 5 gainers for every decliner in early trade. STI +0.1% at 3206.83 midday vs 3220.51 morning high (new year-to-date intraday peak, highest since May 2008), which could provide resistance for rest of day. "The market has come off a bit, with some mixed reaction to the MAS tightening. But a pullback shouldn't be surprising as we had a good run yesterday," says local house trader. Wilmar (F34.SG) top percentage gainer among blue chips, +4.1% at S$6.40, on hopes plantation group will benefit from higher cooking oil prices in China. \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) down 0.7% at S$9.78, SembMarine (S51.SG) off 0.2% at S$4.40; stronger SGD vs USD deemed tad negative for rig builders as bulk of their contracts denominated in USD. Overall volume modest at 1.19 billion shares worth S$1.17 billion. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] After downbeat production report from its 75%-owned \uc2 Coal & Allied\uc2  (CNA.AU) yesterday, \uc2 UBS\uc2  expected production declines and guidance downgrades for \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2 's (RIO.AU) coal production. Thermal coal FY10 guidance came in worse than \uc2 UBS\uc2  estimate, 19.1 million tons vs 19.7 million tons; but most valuable hard coking coal was guided at 9.5 million tons in FY10 vs 9.4 million tons estimate, while 2.4 million tons mined in 3Q came in well above 2.2 million tons estimate. Copper more downbeat, coming in at 159,000 tons mined vs 169,900 estimate. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options down tad at 11.60%/12.30% from 11.65%/12.35% Wednesday in New York as stability in spot early in morning in global day prompted some players to trim USD downside hedges, says options dealer at major Japan bank. But sharp falls in vols unlikely for rest of global day as some demand for USD downside hedges later returns after USD/JPY marks fresh 15-year low at 81.23 in late Tokyo morning trade. Such demand may be sustained ahead amid view Japan PM Kan's comments Wednesday that G-20 countries, including South Korea, China, need to "act responsibly" in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, might have made it difficult for Japan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again. Says earlier in day, before spot fell below 81.50, multiple names bought overnight USD-put/JPY-call options contract with strike price of 81.50 with implied vols of 13.75%, in unknown amounts.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 23,704.70 midday, earlier touching 23,788.94, highest intraday level since June 6, 2008, bolstered by gains in regional markets on Wall Street cue. With index rising for 8th session out of 9, near-term profit-taking likely, but more long-term upside expected. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  raises HSI's end-2011 target to 26,500 from 23,050, to "reflect improved earnings expectations and our revised target multiple of 14.5X (from the historical average of 12.8X)." HKEx (0388.HK) rallies 3.5% to HK$175.80 on sharp increase in HK market volume, which hits HK$78.09 billion at interval; full-day volume may approach HK$150 billion mark, last time HK market had over HK$150 billion volume was January 2008. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0128ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
