sell 3000 reflected average 2-year correlation of 0.50 with Russell India Index and 0.48 with Russell China Index. "While the Russell China Index has correlated to the U.S. market at a relatively steady degree during the 14-year period from September of 1996 through September of 2010, staying within a range of 0.30 and 0.75, India's correlation to the Russell 3000 has increased more so since 2006, reaching a 0.80 correlation point in 2009." (john.kumar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday +0.5% at 3628.477 in moderate volume, off earlier high of 3643.771, led by foreign-buying in most blue chips amid hopes for strong 9-month earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3650. "Overall sentiment however, looks cautious as local institutional funds continue to take profit," says trader at Lautandhana Securities . Foreigners net buyers of IDR180 billion worth of shares. Among gainers; gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) +3.1% at IDR4,125, coal miner Adaro (ADRO.JK) +4.8% at IDR2,200, while Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) also +2.5% at IDR4,100. Profit-taking hits \uc2 XL Axiata\uc2  (EXCL.JK) down 4.3% at IDR5,550. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] As many other developed economies consider further easing of monetary policy, Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands alone in contemplating further rate rises, says Heard on the Street; since RBA left rates on hold last week, surprising many, strong consumer sentiment and jobs data has made a hike next month seem a certainty. But AUD approaching parity with USD, a worry for Australia's exporting industries; already there's talk success of the country's mining sector, on the back of Chinese growth, is causing "Dutch disease" -- the concern being that success of resources industry could dampen prospects for rest of economy. See: HEARD ON THE STREET: Australia's Lonely \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (andrew.peaple@dowjo