\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Foreign Reserves Swell, Highlighting  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Batson and Aaron Back   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 12:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves swelled to $2.648 trillion at the end of September, in a record surge that highlights the extent of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at a time of rising global tensions over exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 The reserves, already by far the largest such stash in the world, jumped by $194.03 billion in the third quarter of the year, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data issued Wednesday show, marking their biggest-ever quarterly gain. Economists said the increase in reserves was driven by both a rebound in China's trade surplus and the recent fall in the U.S. dollar, which increases the value of China's non-dollar reserve holdings in dollar terms.\par \par \uc2 China adds to its reserves whenever the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sells its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. That makes the buildup of reserves one of the main public indicators of the government's policy of limiting the moves in its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The latest jump in reserves--which follows China's pledge of increased exchange-rate flexibility in June--shows that there is still market pressure for the yuan to rise and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still resisting that pressure, economists said.\par \par \uc2 "The huge increase last quarter will make it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to argue that its policy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not a major driver of global imbalances," said Mark Williams of Capital Economics in London.\par \par \uc2 International tensions over exchange rates were at the forefront of last weekend's meetings of the International Monetary Fund in Washington, and are expected to feature prominently at next month's summit of the Group of 20 major economies in Seoul. The prospect of looser U.S. monetary policy has recently helped push the dollar down sharply against a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from the euro and yen to the Australian dollar and Brazilian real, with the notable exception of the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has risen only about 2.3% against the dollar since June, most of that in the last month. Many U.S. politicians say China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still undervalued, and amounts to an unfair advantage for its exporters. Other countries have also complained that China's tightly controlled exchange rate puts more of the burden of adjustment to a weaker dollar on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exporters, letting Chinese manufacturers off easy.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said the U.S. wants to make sure the yuan appreciates "at a gradual but still significant rate."\par \par \uc2 The latest reserve figure "certainly puts a target around the neck of China's exchange-rate policy in the newly declared \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars," said Tom Orlik, an analyst at Stone & McCarthy Research Associates in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 For its part, China fears economic instability from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings, and officials have repeatedly said it will make only "gradual" changes in the exchange rate. In part that is to ensure local companies can adjust, and in part it is to deter waves of speculative capital coming into the country to bet on a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those worries are shared by many other governments, especially in Asia, which has been a favorite of investors seeking better returns amid uncertain economic prospects and low interest rates in much of the developed world. Such inflows have aggravated the upward pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and many countries have been looking for ways to fight back. Earlier this week, Thailand removed a tax break on foreign investment in its government bonds in hopes of stemming the baht's rise.\par \par \uc2 Chinese regulators have also repeatedly signaled concern over inflows of so-called "hot money"--basically meaning speculative capital. "Because of market conditions including the expectation of renminbi appreciation and a positive interest-rate gap with foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this situation of net cross-border fund inflows is unlikely to change," the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a report Tuesday, using the official name for China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest data contain signs that such inflows may have picked up in the last couple of months. The $194 billion quarterly increase in reserves is sizable enough that it can't all be easily accounted for, meaning that other capital inflows could well have contributed.\par \par \uc2 Much of the increase came from improved export earnings: China's trade surplus for the three months to September totaled $65.64 billion, its highest quarterly surplus since the onset of the financial crisis in the fourth quarter of 2008, data issued separately Wednesday show. The trade surplus did, however, fall to a five-month low of $16.88 billion during the single month of September, as commodity imports staged an unexpected rebound. Oil imports, for instance, hit a monthly record in volume terms.\par \par \uc2 Economists think that swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial markets in the third quarter may have added anywhere from $60 billion to $100 billion to the value of China's reserves, which are primarily held in U.S. government and other bonds. That is a reversal from the first half of the year, when the rising U.S. dollar was a drag on the reported value of the reserves.\par \par \uc2 Any capital inflows would add to what look to be increasingly loose monetary conditions within China. Though the government has been trying to contain bank lending within a 7.5 trillion yuan annual target, banks are now lending at a pace that suggests that may be difficult to meet. Banks actually picked up their lending to 595.5 billion yuan in September, from 545.2 billion in August, and have already made a total of 6.3 trillion yuan in new loans so far this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson and Aaron Back, The Wall Street Journal; Andrew.Batson@wsj.com, Aaron.Back@dowjones.com [ 13-10-10 1003GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101013e6ad000u7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
