\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL ECONOMY-Japan questions S.Korea G20 leadership over  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,069 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Japan wants South Korea, China to play by same \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules\par \par \uc2 * Tokyo says its own \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is different\par \par \uc2 * Geithner sees "no risk" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  says G20 should not focus on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Fed says easing may be needed before long (Adds U.S. lawmaker on China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation)\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White and Linda Sieg\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Japan called into question South Korea's leadership of the Group of 20 nations forum on Wednesday because of Seoul's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the won's rise, at the same time insisting its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action was qualitatively different.\par \par \uc2 The remarks by Japan's finance minister underscored deep divisions over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, an issue that will dominate G20 meetings in South Korea this month and next after a weekend International Monetary Fund meeting failed to produce any agreement.\par \par \uc2 "As chair of the G20, South Korea's role will be seriously questioned," Yoshihiko Noda told a parliamentary panel when asked about South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Record low interest rates in rich countries have pushed global investors into emerging markets in search of higher yields, driving up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In response, several governments have stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or tried to curb capital inflows, raising fears of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "race to the bottom" that may trigger protectionism and hobble global growth.\par \par \uc2 Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last month for the first time in more than six years to try to stem a rise in the yen that threatens its fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Noda drew a distinction between that action and more frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korea and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happens regularly, and in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pace of yuan reform has been slow," Noda said. "Our message is that we have confirmed at the Group of Seven that emerging market countries with current account surpluses should allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be more flexible."\par \par \uc2 South Korea did not immediately comment on the remarks.\par \par \uc2 <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic on trade weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2 : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/qun86p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/qun86p}}}\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions map: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/jec96p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/jec96p}}}\par \par \uc2 PDF report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/gez77p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/gez77p}}}\par \par \uc2 For full list of related stories, click [ID:nLDE69308R]\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 NO CONSENSUS\par \par \uc2 Pressure on China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise more quickly is likely to intensify. But hopes for a G20 consensus look slim.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said he saw no risk of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war. But asked about the need for a stronger yuan, he said: "We just want to make sure it's happening at a gradual but still significant rate." For more, see: [ID:nN12218440]\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Senate plans to follow the House of Representatives in passing legislation aimed at pressuring China to let the yuan rise more quickly, Senate Finance Committee Chairman Max Baucus said in the text of a speech given in Beijing. [ID:nN13269803]\par \par \uc2 Ewald Nowotny, a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governing council member, said the G20 was the best forum to discuss the uneven global growth that is leading to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  disputes. [ID:nN13250117]\par \par \uc2 But he said his fear is retaliatory action by the U.S. Congress against China if it fails to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen more rapidly.\par \par \uc2 "What clearly would make us very nervous ... is if some, let's say, unilateral steps would be taken which could spark, let's say, a political crisis. That is something that would be damaging for the world economy in general," he said.\par \par \uc2 China's insistence that the yuan's rise must be gradual is a huge obstacle to the appreciation in Asian exchange rates policymakers say is needed to reduce global imbalances.\par \par \uc2 China and other countries counter that the prospect of the Federal Reserve printing money again will flood the world economy with more liquidity, weaken the dollar and push emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet higher.\par \par \uc2 "It'll be impossible for the G20 to reach a consensus on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many emerging economies feel that they are being forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a weak dollar," said Etsuko Yamashita, chief economist at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "China will not succumb to outside pressure."\par \par \uc2 Minutes of the Fed's last policy meeting showed its policymakers thought easier policy may be needed "before long" to bolster a struggling recovery. [ID:nN12188145]\par \par \uc2 China's chief G20 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  negotiator, Cui Tiankai, said Beijing was trying to avoid a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stand-off but that no specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be on the G20 agenda.\par \par \uc2 "We are doing our best to avoid that," Cui, a foreign vice-minister, said on the sidelines of a conference in Seoul. "But it requires efforts of all the G20 members, not China alone."\par \par \uc2 The major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  not being talked down is the euro, which rose again on Wednesday, as the ECB ponders a reversal of ultra-loose policy while the Fed is poised to ease further and Japan has already cut rates to zero.\par \par \uc2 "In the G4 space, the ECB is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is talking of an exit policy and that is helping the euro," said Ankita Dudani, G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Tokyo's criticism of Seoul stemmed from its worries about competitiveness. The yen is up about 13 percent against the dollar this year, the won only about 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Japan feels it has been under pressure not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of G7 rules but people outside (of G7) seem to be playing by different rules," said Robert Feldman, chief economist at Morgan Stanley MUFG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan urged Seoul and Beijing to act responsibly but acknowledged Tokyo's delicate position.\par \par \uc2 "I want South Korea and China to take responsible actions within common rules, though how to say this is difficult because Japan has also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold 2.1 trillion yen ($26 billion) last month to curb the yen's strength versus the dollar. South Korea has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of about $13 billion since late September, but analysts said it has acted more aggressively in relative terms. (Additional reporting by Yoko Kubota in Tokyo, Cheon Jong-woo in Seoul and David Lawder in Washington; writing by Mike Peacock; editing by Stephen Nisbet and Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 6, GRAPHICS, TV)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|Z|J|ABX|BNX|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101013e6ad0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
