\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: DJIA Closes Above 11000; IMF Meeting Fails To Resolve  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Conflict\b0\par\par\uc2 2,663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 00:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's markets are closed Monday for Health-Sports Day. The U.S. bond market is closed Monday for Columbus Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11006.48  +57.90  +0.53% \par Nasdaq     2401.91  +18.24  +0.77% \par S&P 500    1165.15   +7.09  +0.61% \par FTSE 100   5657.61   -4.52  -0.08% \par Xetra DAX  6291.67  +15.42  +0.25% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 2/32 Unch  0.347% -0.8BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 23/32 +4/32 1.102% -2.5BP \par 10Y Tsy 102 2/32 Unch  2.387% +0.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY       81.95-00     -0.13% \par Range         82.33-81.93 \par EUR/USD       1.3992-94    +0.47% \par Range         1.3996-1.3945 \par AUD/USD       0.9898-99    +0.45% \par Range         0.9898-0.9870 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    12.40-12.70% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    13.55-13.95% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.90-15.20% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,345.30 +$10.30 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,325.60-$1,350.80 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $82.66   +$0.99 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed above 11,000 Friday for the first time since May as a bleak jobs report boosted expectations that the Federal Reserve will move to stimulate economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip index closed up 57.90 points, or 0.53%, to 11006.48, closing above the 11000 level for the first time since May 3, just days before the May 6 "flash crash." The Dow gained 1.6% during the week and is now up 5.5% year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  was the Dow's biggest gainer, surging 5.7% after kicking off earnings season late Thursday with results that exceeded analysts' expectations. The company also lifted its forecast for global aluminum demand in 2010. Caterpillar was ahead 2.1%, \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  rose 1.8% and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  gained 1.7% to help power the Dow's gains.\par \par \uc2 The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite rose 0.77% to 2401.91, adding 1.3% during the week. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index gained 0.61% to 1165.15, with the materials and consumer-discretionary sectors leading the way. The S&P 500 rose 1.7% during the week and is now up 4.5% for 2010.\par \par \uc2 The stock market's gains came after the closely watched nonfarm-payrolls report showed a net 95,000 workers lost their jobs in September, a steeper-than-expected decline. The private sector added 64,000 jobs, while the unemployment rate, which is calculated with a separate household survey, remained unchanged at 9.6% in September.\par \par \uc2 Friday's jobs report served as another reminder that the U.S. labor market remains in the doldrums 15 months after the recession officially ended. But investors hoped Friday that this increases the odds that the Fed will take new steps to jump-start the economy at its next meeting on Nov. 3 by purchasing more Treasurys, known as quantitative easing or "QE2."\par \par \uc2 "It always makes me a little nervous when it seems like the market gets focused on one variable," said John Apruzzese, chief investment officer of Evercore Wealth Management. "It seems to be completely focused on QE2, so negative news is good because it makes it more likely the Fed will do it." That's a bit concerning, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Looking longer term, the QE is not a panacea--if it doesn't work, then we're going to be in serious trouble," he added.\par \par \uc2 St. Louis Federal Reserve Bank President James Bullard said Friday that the Fed would face a difficult decision on whether to begin another round of asset purchases. "The economy has slowed, but it hasn't slowed so much," he said, adding that he would go into the meeting "with an open mind."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at their highest level in five months Friday on the first day of trading after the country's week-long National Day holiday, as rising commodities prices sent metals and mining firms soaring.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade between 2700 and 2800 this week, as funds flow into China's stock market again following the holiday.\par \par \uc2 "Investors were quite cautious before the holiday in light of the possibility that Beijing could hike interest rates or introduce other important measures during the break," said Zhang Guojiang, an analyst at \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  Wealth Management Center. "With no major monetary policy moves and the latest round of property tightening already out, the market is set to take off this month."\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares extended their winning streak to a fifth session Friday, ending at a more than two-year high as the local market tracked gains in mainland China.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the market to consolidate in the near term, but that any retreat is unlikely to be significant, with 22,000 a strong support.\par \par \uc2 "The property tightening measures just announced in Shanghai...may be taken as an excuse for the market to correct," said Castor Pang, a research director at \uc2 Cinda International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Friday on jitters over the strong yen, along with anticipation ahead of upcoming U.S. jobs data and the weekend Group of Seven gathering. The market is closed Monday for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices rebounded in U.S. trading Friday to settle higher, lifted by weakness in the dollar and concerns that a continuing strike at a French oil port may begin crimping supplies.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures rallied Friday as weaker-than-expected U.S. employment figures bumped up expectations of further U.S. monetary easing and sent investors into the metal to hedge a weakening dollar.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange ended sharply higher Friday after the U.S. jobs data raised expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will take measures to stimulate the economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dropped to a 15-year low against the yen Friday, with data showing speculative investors ramped up their pro-yen bets by 72%, as speculation swirled that the Federal Reserve may soon act to stimulate a sagging economy, weighed down further by Friday's weak jobs data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After Friday's weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data, the dollar fell as far as Y81.72, putting investors on alert for signs of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem yen strength; traders said there were no signs of Japan in the market. Japanese Vice Finance Minister Rintaro Tamaki declined to comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also weakened against its other major rivals, as the weak U.S. jobs report "reinforces the view" that another round of Fed asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, could wait in the wings, weighing on the dollar, said Alan Ruskin, global head of G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.3929 from $1.3912 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y82.08 from Y82.40 after having weakened as far as Y81.72. The euro was at Y114.33 from Y114.65. The U.K. pound was at $1.5953 from $1.5862. The dollar was at CHF0.9637 from CHF0.9669.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 77.255 from 77.464.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though data on speculative investments released Friday do not include Friday's price action, they show that investors betting in favor of the yen "are not fearing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , nor the [Bank of Japan] monetary" easing, which was implemented this week in a so-far unsuccessful effort to drive down the yen, said Camilla Sutton, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net speculative bets, called longs, in favor of the yen increased to 49,206 contracts in the week ended Tuesday, compared with the previous week's data that showed 28,666 pro-yen contracts, according to a Scotia Bank analysis of the weekly Commitments of Traders report released by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission late Friday afternoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund's annual meeting this weekend failed to ease \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  battles roiling markets, pushing the dispute off to a summit next month of the Group of 20 countries, with no clear resolution in sight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF meeting Saturday may have been more significant for possible solutions ruled out. Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials rejected calls for an international or regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accord. The World Trade Organization's chief said his institution didn't want to get involved in exchange-rate fights.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IMF members also scotched an effort by the U.S. to link a bigger Chinese role in the IMF to changes in Beijing's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies. "Nobody is linking this," said Egyptian Finance Minister Youssef Boutros-Ghali, who chairs the IMF's policy-making committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A U.S. official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said the Obama administration would take the fight over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a Seoul summit of the leaders of the G-20 industrialized and developing nations in mid-November. The administration used the last G-20 summit in June to prod China to announce beforehand that it would adopt a more "flexible" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, rather than tying the yuan tightly to the dollar. But after the summit, the value of the yuan barely moved, provoking outrage on Capitol Hill and frustration in the Treasury and White House.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, said he figures U.S. and European pressure will diminish once unemployment there begins to decline. He also dismissed the possibility, suggested by some Chinese academics, that Beijing should offer to limit exports to the U.S. temporarily as a way to defuse tensions, in the same way that Japan limited auto exports to the U.S. in the 1980s.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That's an opinion of a small group of economists," Zhou said. "It's not seriously considered." [ 10-10-10 2208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the two sides at loggerheads, IMF Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn said the IMF will take a bigger role in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues. On Friday, he suggested a "systemic stability initiative," in which the IMF would work on a number of fronts to ease \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  battles. But by the end of the IMF meeting on Saturday, he described a narrower effort in which the IMF would focus its reviews of country policies more heavily on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues. He said he also will attend the country-review sessions on China, as he already does with those on the U.S. and the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's weak non-farm payrolls data "simply provided another excuse to sell USD," BNZ Strategist Mike Jones said Monday, adding that there was "little for markets to get excited about from the weekend's IMF/G7 meetings," with lots of chatter about the risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars "but little in the way of coordination or agreement."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jones noted that the IMF agreed to produce a "spillover report," to assess monetary policy, exchange rate, and capital flow linkages among the U.S., China, the U.K., Japan and the euro zone but that analysts are sceptical about what impact the report would have.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys maturing in the next three to seven years were a bit stronger Friday after a bleak U.S. jobs report added to already lofty expectations that the Federal Reserve will have to lend more support to the economy this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yields on two- and five-year notes fell to record lows once again in the wake of the report, which showed the economy lost 95,000 jobs last month, to 0.335% and 1.070%, respectively. The 10-year note's yield slipped to a yearly low as well, to 2.332%, a level last seen in January 2009. The 10-year note's yield hit an all-time low of 2.037% in December 2008. Yields and prices move inversely. All issues but the 30-year Treasury bond were left with a weekly gain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices pushed up as the employment report confirmed for many that the Fed will have to launch another quantitative-easing program involving large-scale bond buying this year to keep the economy from slipping back into a recession. Many believe that buying will take Treasury yields lower still, with the 10-year note's yield possibly hitting 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains were tame, though, ahead of supply in the coming week. The government will sell $66 billion in three-, 10- and 30-year Treasurys Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The employment report "gives us no reason to argue a slowing of the QE2 train based on the data," said John Briggs, U.S. interest-rate strategist at RBS. "Akin to the overall growth picture, today's data was 'just not good enough'," Briggs said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Friday afternoon trading in New York, the five-year note was up 4/32 to yield 1.102% and the 10-year note was flat to yield 2.387%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond was under pressure Friday, as it has been recently; investors are expecting the Fed to buy short-term Treasurys rather than longer-term ones. The 30-year bond was off by 24/32 to yield 3.746%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. bond market is closed Monday for the Columbus Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Monday, Malaysia's industrial output likely rose 5.0% on the year in August, up from July's 3.2% rise, according to the median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Loses 95K Jobs In September; Jobless Rate 9.6% \par Geithner Says Rebalancing Not Progressing As Needed \par Bank Of America Halts All Foreclosures \par AIG Begins Exchange Offer Of Up To 74.5M Units \par Crop Report Ripples Through Agriculture Sector \par IMF Chief Says More Stimulus Needed \par Nasdaq Will Remove Hidden Order Data \par =WSJ: IMF Meeting Fails To Resolve Conflict Over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par Japan Fin Min: Didn't Discuss Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  With US Geithner \par =WSJ: Europe Would Effectively Cede 2 Seats On IMF Board - IMF Official \par IMF Panel: Fund Should Deepen Work On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Capital Flows, Reserves \par IMF Report Outlines Strategy, Hurdles \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Proposal \par PBOC Gov: Hope Yuan To Get Stronger With Economic Development \par PBOC: China Aims For Current Acct Surplus Below 4% Of GDP In 3-5 Yrs \par Japan Fin Min:Rich Nations' Monetary Easing Bring On 'New Risks' \par Japan Finance Minister Renews Threat Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par EU's Rehn: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  'Encouraged' To Make Yuan More Flexible Soon \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 GDF Suez\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Supply Cnooc 2.6 Mln Tons LNG Over Four Years \par China SAFE: End-June Foreign Debt $513.81 Bln \par PBOC Zhou: Urges Countries To Ensure Stable Financial Systems \par PBOC Gov: Understand Pressure On Yuan Surfaces During Difficult Econ Recovery \par S Korea September Producer Prices Rise At Fastest Pace In 14 Months \par ECB Bini Smaghi: IMF Mulling Pushing Back Greek Loan Repayment Date \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0000  US           Kansas City Fed Pres Hoenig gives lecture on William \par                    Taylor, who played critical role in banking crises of 1980s \par 0030  Australia    Aug Housing Finance \par 0400  Malaysia     Aug Manufacturing sales \par 0400  Malaysia     Aug Industrial Production Index \par 0645  France       Aug Industrial Production Index \par 0830  UK           Sep Bank of England narrow money \par 1000  France       Aug OECD Composite Leading Indicators \par 1200  US           New York Fed Pres Dudley speaks in Washington \par 1845  US           Fed Vice Chair Yellen speaks on 'Mile-High Challenges to \par                    Economic Prosperity' in Denver \par 2301  UK           Sep BRC-KPMG Retail Sales Monitor \par 2301  UK           Sep RICS UK Housing Market Survey \par N/A   New Zealand  Sep Residential Property Value Growth \par N/A   US           BoE Mervyn King at the Mortimer Caplin Conference on the World \par                    Economy \par N/A   Germany      Aug Balance of Payments \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozoli