\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ TALK BACK: Jobs Growth Is Stalled; New Fed Policies Won't Help\b0\par\par\uc2 1,004 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 16:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 These are the personal views of Peter Morici, a professor at the University of Maryland's Robert H. Smith School of Business and former chief economist at the U.S. International Trade Commission:\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Labor Department will report September employment, and forecasters expect the economy added zero new jobs. Unemployment is projected to rise a notch to 9.7%.\par \par \uc2 Completion of temporary jobs cloud the picture, subtracting an expected 75,000 positions, but the Labor Department is likely to show that the private sector added about the same number of jobs; core private sector employment--private jobs, less government-subsidized health care and social services, and temporary services--likely added a paltry 35,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Core private wage earners must be added to pay taxes for new positions mandated by new federally subsidized health benefits, financial sector regulations, and alternative energy technologies, or the federal deficit will fly into orbit; nevertheless, new tax-paying jobs are simply not materializing in sufficient numbers.\par \par \uc2 By the end of 2013, 13 million private sector jobs must be added to bring unemployment down to 6%, and Obama administration policies are not creating conditions for businesses to hire 333,000 workers each month.\par \par \uc2 Since the recovery began in July 2009, demand for U.S.-made goods and services has increased at a 1.2% annual pace. Potential demand growth is about 5% but the economic stimulus package, health care, and bank reforms, along with policies to bolster alternative energy and conservation, have not delivered the jolt to growth the administration promised.\par \par \uc2 In the second quarter, consumer spending; investment in new structures, equipment and software; and government purchases added 4.4% to demand. However, imports grew much more rapidly than exports, and the trade deficit tapped 3.5% off demand. The difference, about 0.9%, was growth in demand for U.S.-made goods and services.\par \par \uc2 Businesses can accommodate up to 2% growth by boosting productivity without adding workers. Unless rapid growth in imports can be curbed, the U.S. economy is headed for a very slow expansion, with unemployment permanently near 10% or higher.\par \par \uc2 Permanent increases in federal spending and regulatory burdens are further encouraging outsourcing and discouraging hiring.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the 2008 crisis, President George W. Bush spent 19.6% of GDP and the deficit was $161 billion, whereas President Barack Obama's budget projects outlays at 25.1% of GDP and a $1.3 trillion deficit in 2011, assuming the economy is growing at 4%. Most economists just don't see growth that robust, and the government's share and federal deficits are likely to be much greater than Obama's economic team is projecting.\par \par \uc2 Too much spending will require too many new taxes and not just pushing rates marginally above 50% on families earning $250,000. Fears about new taxes and more regulations are stifling business investment and further encouraging outsourcing.\par \par \uc2 Without addressing oil and China, along with curbs on government spending and smarter regulatory reforms, other efforts to create jobs are futile.\par \par \uc2 The moratorium on deepwater drilling, though popular with environmentalists, kills jobs in petroleum and supporting industries and sends too many consumer dollars abroad that could be spent here, creating U.S. jobs.\par \par \uc2 Detroit has the technology to build much more efficient and attractive gasoline-powered vehicles now, and a national policy to rapidly replace the existing fleet would reduce oil imports and create many high-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 China purposefully undervalues the yuan, making its products artificially cheap and deceivingly competitive on U.S. and European store shelves, and Beijing's promises of new flexibility on exchange rates have not translated into meaningful actions.\par \par \uc2 Beijing prints billions of yuan to purchase dollars and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to push down the value of the yuan, creating a 35% subsidy on exports. Beijing's purchases exceed $450 billion annually.\par \par \uc2 Without progress on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by China, Japan and others, which essentially fix exchange rates, efforts to jump-start growth through "quantitative easing" and other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tricks won't work. Those won't change the consequences of the aggregate trade deficit of Western economies via Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators; in fact, it could make those deficits worse.\par \par \uc2 As Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke wrote with co-author Andrew Abel in their macroeconomics textbook, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operating under fixed exchange rates "cannot use monetary policy to pursue macroeconomic stabilization goals."\par \par \uc2 Unless Obama stands up to China on exchange rates, Bernanke knows his options are severely constrained.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. tax on conversion of dollars into yuan could be set to just offset the subsidies Beijing provides Chinese producers exporting into the U.S., causing prices of Chinese products to reflect their true cost to the U.S. economy. That would stimulate U.S. manufacturing and supplying industries more than any spending package Congress could put together.\par \par \uc2 Simply, the tax could be set to equal China's official purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  divided by its exports, currently about 35%, and be phased out as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduces its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lets the value of the yuan rise.\par \par \uc2 If Obama wants to fix the federal deficit and create jobs, perhaps he should spend less, get serious about better using and developing U.S. energy resources, and implement a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tax.\par \par \uc2 Then newspapers could treat their readers to stories about the American industrial renaissance.\par \par \uc2 (The author can be reached at pmorici@rhsmith.umd.edu.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. Talk Back comments can be found under the N/TLK code.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2010 10:17 ET (14:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101006e6a6003ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
