\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 959 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 07:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA'S OFFICIAL SEPTEMBER PMI UP, EASING SLOWDOWN WORRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing activity expanded in September and accelerated from the previous month, an official gauge showed Friday, adding to evidence that Chinese growth remains robust and that its slowdown from extraordinary growth rates earlier this year will be mild. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: JAPAN CPI FALLS FOR 18TH MONTH; DEFLATION PERSISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's consumer prices fell in August for the 18th consecutive month, government data showed Friday, suggesting no end in sight for a deflationary trend that has been undermining the broader economy, and providing further impetus for the Bank of Japan to take additional monetary easing measures soon. (published 0458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER RENEWS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister renewed his pledge Friday to take "decisive" steps to fight the yen's rise, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edges back toward last month's 15-year high against the dollar that triggered Tokyo's first market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in more than six years. (published 0249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ISSUES JAPAN TRAVEL WARNING CITING ALLEGED ATTACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has issued a travel warning for Japan, reflecting continued strains between the two neighbors despite efforts to ratchet down tensions. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: SOUTH KOREA CONSUMER PRICES HIT 17-MONTH HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's consumer inflation shot to a 17-month high in September, touching the midpoint of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2% to 4% inflation target band for the first time since January. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: S KOREA EXPORTS UP 17.2% ON YEAR IN SEPTEMBER VS +13.5% FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports grew faster than expected in September despite widespread concerns over the health of the global economic recovery, boosting the nation's trade surplus to $5.01 billion from $1.72 billion previously, according to the preliminary trade data from the Ministry of Knowledge Economy Friday. (published 0002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVERNMENT OFFICIAL: ANNUAL INFLATION LIKELY LOW-3% IN 4Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unexpected jump in South Korea's inflation rate in September stems mainly from a temporary lack of supply of agricultural products due to heavier than usual rain in summer, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Friday. (published 0103 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA SEPTEMBER \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  MANUFACTURING PMI 48.8 VS 50.9 IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's manufacturing sector slowed for a fifth straight month in September, with a key gauge measuring activity falling below the neutral level for the first time in 19 months, amid escalating concerns over the strength of the global economic recovery. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA TO REMOVE TARIFFS ON CABBAGE, RADISH IMPORTS BY END-2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea will temporarily remove its tariffs on cabbage and radish imports and buy 150 tons of the fresh vegetables from China in an effort to help curb soaring fresh vegetable prices, the Ministry of Food, Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries said Friday. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA SEPTEMBER PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX -4.4 POINTS TO 47.3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector contracted in September for the first time this year as factory production was hit hard by a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rising costs, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Friday shows. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: INDONESIA SEPT CPI +0.44% ON MO; +5.80% ON YR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in Indonesia eased in September, giving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more room to hold off from raising interest rates. (published 0320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: INDONESIA AUG TRADE SURPLUS $1.49B AS EXPORTS HIT NEW HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia recorded a trade surplus of $1.49 billion in August, turning around from a deficit of $130 million in July, as exports rose to a new record high, the Central Statistics Agency said Friday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: THAI SEPTEMBER CPI UP 3.0% ON YEAR VS EXPECTED 3.3% RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's consumer price index rose 3.0% in September from a year earlier, compared with a 3.3% increase in August, the Commerce Ministry's Permanent Secretary Yanyong Phuangrach said Friday. (published 0329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : COMMERCIAL BANKS' JAN-AUG LOANS UP 7% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday loans of commercial banks grew 7% on year during the first eight months of this year, and the outlook for loan growth for the rest of the year remains positive, in line with the improving domestic economy. (published 0449 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE 3Q PRIVATE HOME PRICES UP 3.1% VS 5.3% IN 2Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's private residential-property prices rose 3.1% in the third quarter from the previous three months, slowing from the second quarter's 5.3% rise, according to flash estimates issued by the government Friday. (published 0436 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FED'S PIANALTO POINTS TO SIGNS ECONOMY IS STARTING TO MEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there are signs the U.S. is starting to recover from the deepest downturn since the Great Depression, the Federal Reserve has options if the economy ends up needing more help to heal, Cleveland Federal Reserve President Sandra Pianalto said Thursday. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: TREASURY TO SELL CITIGROUP TRUST SECURITIES FOR $2.25B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury Department Thursday moved to sell down its \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . holdings, the latest government effort to unwind the hefty ownership stakes it took in financial institutions at the height of the financial crisis. (published 2320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS DECLINE ON SLIGHT SPOT MOVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen options edged down Friday, as the underlying exchange rate stayed in a narrow range, but further falls appear unlikely as bargain-hunting may emerge below current low levels. (published 0420 GMT) [ 01-10-10 0503GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a10008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
