\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD - the view from Reuters Asia news editors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2010\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Following is the view from Reuters Asia editors on the news that is likely to matter most this week:\par \par \uc2 ASIA MARKETS OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 With the focus on how soon the Fed could launch its second round of quantitative easing, it's easy to forget Asia's role in providing liquidity to global markets. Most central banks in the region have, indeed, started raising interest rates. Allowing a certain amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength has also been a tightening tool. But stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks has led to an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that appears to be being funneled back into U.S. Treasuries, besides the usual minor degree of diversification. Just look at the increased accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the past few months. And then look at the soaring increase in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings of Treasuries, now running at close to the record levels reached in the post-Lehman environment when everyone was scrambling to hold dollars. This comes just as dollar-centric Japan has started an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign. While the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have gone silent since the initial burst of yen-selling totaling about $23 billion, just about every market participant expects them to step back in. The dollar is struggling, and emerging market central banks are opting to block the upward pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars and recycling a sizable chunk of those back into Treasuries. Asian authorities are starting to see some leveling out of export growth, perhaps making them more cautious on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. So even before the Fed makes any decision on boosting its own Treasury purchases, the dollar's slide is prompting Asian and other central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then do the same. That liquidity injection and the resulting downward pressure on Treasury yields may not yet be helping to kindle solid growth or employment in the United States. But it does mean there are plenty of investors scouring the world looking for returns and pouring that money into emerging markets. (eric.burroughs@thomsonreuters.com) > Five world markets themes this week [MKT/THEMES] > MARKETS WEEKAHEAD Cash flood reshapes investment [ID:nLDE68N0G7]\par \par \uc2 REUTERS INSIDER WEEKAHEAD\par \par \uc2 A heavy data week for Asia, with Japanese trade figures kicking off the action on Monday while the focus is also on yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prospects. Also on tap: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  manufacturing data, and Asian inflation.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/tax25p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/tax25p}}}\par \par \uc2 ASIA MANUFACTURING UPDATE\par \par \uc2 We get \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMIs for China, India and Korea on Friday, alongside China's official PMI. Trade data for India, Indonesia, Japan and Korea also due this week. The PMI series of leading indicators comes at a crucial time, when the region's fastest growing economies are seeing the much anticipated moderation from a V-shaped recovery. The strength in industrial output in China and India notwithstanding, we should see weaker retail sales in the United States, a let-up in inventory building and the wearing-off of base effects show up in weaker growth numbers in Asia. The weakness in China's imports should be visible in export numbers at its neighbours, although base effects do play a part in that and there seems to be a cushion building up in Asia-ex-China regional trade. (vidya.ranganathan@thomsonreuters.com) > Reuters polls and surveys [ID:nL10903477] > Top economic events [M/DIARY]\par \par \uc2 BUFFETT & GATES IN CHINA\par \par \uc2 Warren Buffett and Bill Gates tour China, with three stops during the week, all involving Buffett-invested carmaker BYD . First stop is Monday (27), when the pair visits BYD's headquarters in Shenzhen. The pair will then travel to Beijing on Wednesday (29) for the launch of a new model, and then will end their trip with a stop in the central China city of Changsha on Thursday (30) to tour a BYD electric bus plant. (jean.yoon@thomsonreuters.com) > Company news diary [GLO/EQUITY] > Asia company earnings [ASIA/EQTY]\par \par \uc2 BHP'S $39 BLN BID FOR POTASH\par \par \uc2 China's Sinochem Corp is in early talks with banks to finance its likely bid for all or part of \uc2 Potash Corp\uc2  of Canada , the South China Morning Post newspaper reported on Saturday, in a sign China is serious about derailing \uc2 BHP Billiton Ltd\uc2 's $39 billion hostile bid. \uc2 Sinochem\uc2  has engaged \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  and Citi to look at ways of thwarting BHP's bid. In North America, focus expected to be on Potash's lawsuit against BHP bid. (jean.yoon@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan governor Masaaki Shirakawa speaks on Monday, BOJ Tankan on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The yen is now central, both to business sentiment and to BOJ policy, and this week we find out how far its strength is eating into plans at big firms for capital expenditure through December and influencing what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do on Oct 4-5. What we do know now is that the BOJ is inching closer to quantitative easing as it lets banks' current account balances build up, but is still loath to admit that.\par \par \uc2 For a PDF package that provides an in-depth look at the market and macro implications of yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including trading strategies and graphics on the yen's strength and cross-market correlations, click: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/fac44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/fac44p}}}\uc2  (vidya.ranganathan@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 CHINA SHOWDOWN WITH WASHINGTON OVER YUAN\par \par \uc2 A U.S. congressional panel turned up the pressure on China over the yuan last week, approving a bill that would let the United States slap duties on goods from countries with undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The legislation has been sent to the full House for a vote next week. It may never become law, however, because even if -- as seems likely -- it passes the House in a floor vote next Wednesday, there is little chance it will pass the Senate this session. While it is in the interests of neither side to allow a trade war to erupt, U.S. legislators have an eye on November congressional elections and for domestic political reasons many want to be seen to be taking a tough line with China.\par \par \uc2 Reuters explains the factors behind the yuan's sudden gains, the U.S. legislation being considered and the factors driving China's policy in a PDF: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/haz94p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/haz94p}}}\uc2  (john.chalmers@thomsonreuters.com) > Five key Asia political risk themes to watch [ID:nSGE65501O]\par \par \uc2 CHINA, JAPAN ROW SIMMERS\par \par \uc2 Tokyo sought to defuse an escalating confrontation with Beijing at the end of last week by releasing the captain of a Chinese trawler that had collided with two Japanese patrol boats in disputed waters. However, the row dragged on through the weekend, with China demanding an apology from its neighbour for detaining the captain and Japan refusing to offer one. (john.chalmers@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 ASIA POLITICAL RISKS\par \par \uc2 For a roundup of the key political risk themes to watch in Asia this week, plus links to related Reuters stories and country risk factboxes, please click:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/jum59n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/jum59n}}}\par \par \uc2 AIA KICKS OFF $15 BLN IPO PREMARKETING\par \par \uc2 AIA management will kick off pre-marketing to gauge demand from institutions. We expect the names of cornerstone investors between premarketing and the road shows of its roughly $15 billion IPO of AIA. (jean.yoon@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 GRAINS CONFERENCE\par \par \uc2 Rising grain prices, fuelled by supply concerns, will top the agenda at this week's conference organised by the United Nations' food agency as it seeks to find a balance between exporters and importers to rein in spiralling prices. The Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) is hosting a week-long meeting in the South Korean city of Gyeongju amid volatility in the grain markets. Grain prices have jumped to multi-year highs since the spring but they have not reached the level of 2008 when just about every asset, including commodities, soared on speculative buying, and there are no clear signs new highs will be scaled. (sambit.mohanty@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 GAMES FIASCO\par \par \uc2 India is struggling to salvage the Commonwealth Games with workers racing to get venues and accommodation ready and more athletes pulling out due to concerns over security, shoddy construction, unsanitary accommodation and a dengue fever epidemic. While the country's image has been damaged by the fiasco, and will take an even bigger hit if the Games are cancelled, this is unlikely to be a significant deterrent to foreign investors, who have long been aware of India's drawbacks but are still attracted by its compelling strengths - in particular a large and growing domestic consumer base. (john.chalmers@thomsonreuters.com) (Editing by Dean Yates)\par \par \uc2 WEEKAHEAD/ASIA|LANGEN|Table|G|RBN|Z|J|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100926e69q0006t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
