\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Fed Joins Other Central Banks In No-Win  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Game\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 20:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Federal Reserve just cemented its position as the leader among world central banks softening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although the Fed would be very unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as Japanese monetary authorities did last week, it is still rooting for a weaker dollar. That's one upshot of Tuesday's statement in which the Federal Open Market Committee said that U.S. inflation is lower than the Fed's mandate permits, thus implying a willingness to take more credit-easing measures if needed.\par \par \uc2 More critical commentators would argue that by keeping the door open to even more liquidity injections, following the past year's $1.7 trillion asset-buying program, the Fed is debasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And certainly, the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially rallied against the greenback on Tuesday's announcement.\par \par \uc2 Weakening the dollar wouldn't be a bad policy for fighting deflation in a perfectly balanced world. But the problem is that everybody else is worried about a loss of competitiveness and wants to debase their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too. Japan is not the only one.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying its member countries' sovereign bonds and accepting banks' dubious collateral in return for a cheap, long-term credit facility, all of which inherently softens the euro.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, emerging Asian and Latin American countries are turning more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt their exporters.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  estimates, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $18.6 billion to weaken the real in the eight months to August, up from $7.3 billion in the same period last year.\par \par \uc2 Similar steps have been repeatedly taken by the Bank of Korea and Southeast Asian central banks.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the mother of all \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, with its explicit policy of keeping the yuan undervalued.\par \par \uc2 We've also seen record-breaking dollar and euro purchases by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  as the Swiss franc has rallied on safe-haven inflows this year. Just last week, the SNB presented an extremely dovish outlook statement.\par \par \uc2 Even the Reserve Bank of New Zealand turned against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, with an unexpectedly cautious outlook statement. That leaves the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  as virtually the only hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left.\par \par \uc2 This has to end in tears. Competitive devaluations cannot work for everyone. It's simple math: when one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises, another must fall.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 /easy money game is making it hard for traders to pick appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's one reason why gold keeps hitting record highs. Often treated as an \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alternative, gold is absorbing buying activity that might otherwise go to high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but can't because of these activist central banks. Moreover, many gold fans believe these policies will eventually stoke global hyperinflation, for which the precious metal is a traditional hedge.\par \par \uc2 Yet, just as paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' exchange rates are capped by their international counterparts' inability to fall to zero, gold's gains will eventually be limited by the dollar's own downside limits. Gold has very little industrial use and under the current monetary system, investors wanting to convert their gold portfolio into something of real value must first transfer back into paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To position for significantly higher gold prices over the long run demands a very bold strategic bet: that the global monetary system as we know it will completely break down and be replaced with a gold standard. With the competitive devaluations doing damage to that system, this might seem like a good call right now, which is why gold does look headed toward $1,300 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Still, this big bet also requires believing that governments will eventually get religion and give up on fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And if anybody has little faith in government, it is the gold bugs.\par \par \uc2 Alternatively, you can choose to believe, equally optimistically, that governments will recognize the self-destructiveness of this race to the bottom and look for a multilateral solution to the underlying economic problem of global demand and supply imbalances.\par \par \uc2 Yet even if that utopian result were likely, it doesn't make it any easier right now to decide which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy. Paper or metal?\par \par \uc2 (Michael Casey, a special writer with Dow Jones Newswires, writes a regular column about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income markets. Previously, he was Newswires' Buenos Aires bureau chief and, before that, assistant managing editor for the U.S. economy, Treasurys and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  group in New York. He can be reached at 212-416-2209 or michael.j.casey@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-09-10 1852GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100921e69l00198\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
