\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(9/17) Push On Yen Aligns Japan With Asia Neighbors\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A12\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Alex Frangos \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG -- Japan launched its battle against a strong yen this week, joining other Asian governments facing a common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  predicament: competition with China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 While attention was focused on the yen's 15-year high against the dollar as Japan began \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, the yen was also near its highest point against the Chinese yuan in a decade.\par \par \uc2 That puts Japan -- an advanced economy -- in the same boat as such export-dependent economies as South Korea, Thailand and Taiwan, which both compete and trade with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and find it necessary to routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to adjust their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , taking the yuan into account.\par \par \uc2 "China is the common denominator in that it's the largest competitor and in some cases the largest trading partner in Asia," says Simon Flint, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Nomura in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 When China allows its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate, as it has in the past few days, that gives room to other Asian economies to tolerate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises. The yuan ended trading Thursday in Shanghai at 6.7248 per dollar, its strongest close since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began trading in 1994, after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the reference rate for daily trading at a fresh high for the fifth straight day. The yuan has risen 1.5% against the dollar since June.\par \par \uc2 In reality, Asia's economies outside China have seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise in anticipation of, rather than in reaction to, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. Since China signaled a change in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on June 19, investors have sent money into other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hoping to ride the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wave higher, even as the yuan has stayed mostly still.\par \par \uc2 South Korea, Thailand and Singapore have all seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise about 3% against the yuan since the June announcement, exacerbating worries about export competitiveness. All three, in addition to Taiwan, have been active in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying dollars and pushing their reserves to record or near-record levels. Asian countries outside China hold $2.9 trillion in reserves. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has $2.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 These countries have decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to analysts, in part because not doing so would cause their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise and make them less competitive in relation to China. In Japan's case, China and the U.S. are the most important trade partners, with China receiving 18% of its exports, while the U.S. accounts for 16%. While Japan had been staying out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with general agreement among Group of Seven countries, some economists see it as one of Tokyo's few options to protect its fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Asia thrived over the past decade by relying on export-oriented growth aided by depressed exchange rates. Some moves are under way in some Asian countries to boost domestic demand and reduce their reliance on exports, but the impact is still limited despite widespread agreement that such a shift is necessary.\par \par \uc2 "Relative exchange rates do matter for competitiveness," Mr. Flint says. "In Asia in general, export-led growth has been very successful, and it's difficult to ignore a winning formula."\par \par \uc2 China has also affected Asian countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy decisions by accelerating the diversification of is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves out of the dollar and into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its neighbors. While China keeps a tight wrap on the composition of its holdings, South Korea and Japan have reported in recent months sharply increased buying by China of government debt in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That has put pressure on those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise, and raised the political temperature in Asia.\par \par \uc2 "Inflows from China's accumulation of Japanese government debt have likely contributed to yen appreciation," Callum Henderson, global head of Standard Chartered's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research, wrote in a research note. He says while China has become more flexible on the yuan and allowed some appreciation, by moving into Japanese bonds, China has opened "a new chapter in the countries' diplomatic and financial relationship."\par \par \uc2 HSBC\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Richard Yetsenga figures other Asian central banks have, like \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , moved reserves accumulated during \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from dollars into yen, in a way passing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  torch around the region. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from one country is begetting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in another," he says.\par \par \uc2 Japan may not be as successful as other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionists\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however. It has a much bigger fight on its hands given that the yen market is among the world's largest, with daily turnover of $568 billion, the Bank for International Settlements says.\par \par \uc2 "The reason \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reasonably effective in Asia outside Japan is because of relative market power. They are not particularly liquid markets, so \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can have a disproportionately large impact," Mr. Flint said.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 16, 2010 19:59 ET (23:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100916e69g0060l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
