\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Euro Gains; Yen Remains Down After  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 20:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2:23 (Dow Jones) Euro climbs to its strongest level against the dollar in more than a month on the heels of a well-received auction of Spanish government debt and amid speculation the US will initiate another round of asset purchases. The dollar, meanwhile, held onto its sharp gains against the yen a day after Japan's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strangle the recent strength in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan rose to the strongest level since it began trading in 1994, the fourth consecutive session in which the yuan hit a record intraday high, on a day when Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said in Congressional testimony China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate has been "too slow." (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:09 (Dow Jones) Treasurys lower in afternoon trading, weighed by debt supply in other parts of the credit markets as well as easing speculation on larger scale bond purchases from the Fed. The selling eases in recent trading, with prices bouncing off session lows. Some hedges tied to new debt supply, which had weakened Treasurys earlier, unwind. Recently, the benchmark 10-year note's price is 6/32 lower to yield 2.744%. The 30-year bond is 15/32 lower to yield 3.903%. Meanwhile, equities stuck in slightly negative territory. DJIA down 4, S&P 500 down 4.(min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:03 (Dow Jones) P.F. Chang's (PFCB) slips as much as 8.5% as investors sort through confusion tied to its roadshow with William Blair. Speculation swirled this morning that company said comparable-store sales would miss 3Q estimates, but William Blair says company made no such comments and in fact didn't address sales or earnings at all. Analyst adds firm has no plans to adjust any estimates. Telsey Advisory also believes reaction is overblown, as PFCB traditionally very conservative in talking about its projections. PFCB not immediately available. Shares off 5.4% to $45.19 on heavy volume. (adam.cancryn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:48 (Dow Jones) Clean sweep so far for the three big asset classes, as US stocks, Treasurys and the dollar are all marking declines. That sort of negative trifecta usually isn't a good sign, but the positive takeaway is that it's far from out of hand. Stocks have been hovering just slightly lower all session, USD index has trimmed earlier declines and Treasurys are moderately lower. Financials, energy and industrials among the sectors declining most. Tech, materials show small gains. DJIA down 12, has remained within a 53-point band from session high to low. Nasdaq off 9, S&P 500 down 4.5. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:41 (Dow Jones) \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's (T) wireless network would be improving even faster, if it could just get those darned parts, phone giant's Business Solutions division head, \uc2 John Stankey\uc2 , tells BofA/Merrill conference. Plans for $2B in added wireless-infrastructure capital expenditures this year are still "suppressed," he says, by $300M in equipment \uc2 AT&T\uc2  wants but can't yet get. Appears supply-chain issues lasting longer than it expected. In May, Stankey told Dow Jones Newswires situation had begun to improve, should ease by summer's end and return to normal. Now, it sounds like issues will last well into fall, at least. (maxwell.murphy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:33 (Dow Jones) For-profit educators surge as popular BMO industry conference generates some much-needed positive sentiment for beaten-down sector. BMO's Jeff Silber notes with a large captive audience, today a good chance to get the positive points across. "Hopefully that gets to Washington as well, to the policymakers and legislators." While companies presenting remain mum on specific effects of DOE's proposed gainful employment rules, Silber says main message from is the companies will figure out a way to adjust their business models. DeVry (DV) CEO notes Obama's higher-ed goals not possible without strong private sector. Corinthian Colleges (COCO) up 9%; Career Education (CECO) rises 5.6%; Strayer Education (STRA) up 4.6%. (caitlin.nish@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:25 (Dow Jones) Apple (AAPL) will begin selling iPad in China and Latin America tomorrow, a move that likely accelerate sales of the already popular device. According to AAPL's Spanish-language website, iPad's Wi-Fi and 3G wireless models go on sale in Argentina, Chile, Colombia, Ecuador and Peru as well as previously announced China. AAPL has sold more than 3.2M iPads since its April debut, and could sell nearly 30M units next year, according to some analysts. International expansion was credited for lifting iPhone sales in previous quarters. AAPL up 0.7% to $272.12. (ian.sherr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:23 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  (JPM) finally issues a formal apology for the web problems that plagued its online banking service earlier this week. "We are sorry for the difficulties that recently affected Chase.com, and we apologize for not communicating better with you during this issue," JPM says on its website. "This was not the level of service we know you expect, and we will work hard to serve you better in the future and to communicate with you better if a situation like this should arise again." The apology is notable as many bloggers and folks on Twitter had criticized JPM for its failure to properly communicate this issue with its customers. (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chase.com/index.jsp?pg_name=ccpmapp/shared/marketing/page/outage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.chase.com/index.jsp?pg_name=ccpmapp/shared/marketing/page/outage}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1:16 (Dow Jones) \uc2 American Equity Investment Life Holding\uc2  (AEL) taking a beating this session, but analysts line up to defend their bullish ratings. Investors seem not to like the news that AEL will issue $150M in senior convertible notes to pay back debt. Sandler O'Neill says using proceeds to pay off credit facility is "incremental positive," and keeps a buy rating. Raymond James affirms strong buy rating, says bottom line is dilution to earnings is in-line with target levels. Shares off 8% at $10 today; AEL up 34% in 2010. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:11 (Dow Jones) Colombian peso weakens as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a bid to limit the appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The peso trades at COP1,813.35 from COP1,807.1 Wednesday. "The market has been extremely calm and stable," says Edgar Romero, an analyst with local brokerage firm Ultrabursatiles. The yield on the benchmark peso-denominated bond stands at 7.207% from 7.273% Wednesday. The IGBC stock index declines 0.2% to 14066. (darcy.crowe@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:08 (Dow Jones) Beyond the confidence-boosting news of a successful Spanish government debt auction, making further sense of the euro's rise Thursday has inspired different interpretations. Its strong performance has been taken by some to reflect more of an anti-dollar sentiment versus a pro-euro conviction. While challenged long-term, there are though broad undercurrents that come out on the euro's favor, says Steven Englander of Citi \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To some, "the combination of euro zone fiscal austerity and ECB reluctant backstopping of sovereign debt is more attractive than the US policy mix of easy fiscal and possibly aggressive quantitative easing," he says. Another pro-euro plus is how liquid it is, versus other rivals, he says. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:04 (Dow Jones) Sprint's (S) 4G network is due for a New York launch the end of the year. Matt Carter, president of Sprint's 4G business unit, won't reveal date for the launch, but it will come with an appropriate marketing campaign. New York's "an important market for us to establish our leadership," he says at media briefing in NYC. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-16-10 1423ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100916e69g000i7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
