\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: $61M Outstanding At NY Fed  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Swap Facility\b0\par\par\uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2010 GMT [Dow Jones] The New York Fed's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap program remains lightly utilized. The ECB rolled over the $60 million it drew a week ago, and the Bank of Japan saw $3 million of the $4 million it borrowed maturing, leaving total outstanding at $61 million. Demand for dollar liquidity among the world's major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appears to be without problems, hence the minimal activity. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2004 GMT [Dow Jones] Asset-backed securities valued at $74.87B have been sold versus $104.5B last year this time, according to Citi data. Auto sector issuance comprises 55% of the total, at $41.21B. Last year by this time, it was about 40% of total at $41.87B. The auto sector of the asset-backed market has come to life this week. \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  sold a $1.3B bond, \uc2 BMW\uc2  sold a $1B security and \uc2 AmeriCredit\uc2  Auto Receivables Trust priced a $850M bond. There was robust demand for all these new bonds and most were increased in size.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2004 GMT [Dow Jones] Hedging from corporate and agency bond sales take down long-term Tsy futures prices. Also, competition from the offerings weigh on contracts tied to lower-yielding Tsys. Dec 10-yr Tsy notes settled down 9/32 at 123-29, near 2.75% cash yield equivalent. Dec 30-yr Tsy bonds settled 25/32 lower at 129-28. Dec ultra long Tsy bonds settled at 135-31, down 31/32. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2001 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wrap: The euro climbed to its strongest level against the dollar in more than a month on the heels of a well-received auction of Spanish government debt and amid speculation the U.S. will initiate another round of economy-stimulating asset purchases. The dollar, meanwhile, held onto its sharp gains against the yen a day after Japan's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strangle the recent strength in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan rose to the strongest level since it began trading in 1994, the fourth consecutive session in which the yuan hit a record intraday high, on a day when Tsy Sec Geithner said in Congressional testimony China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate has been "too slow."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2000 GMT [Dow Jones] Lingering worries about a slowing economy provided enough ammunition for fed-funds futures traders to continue paring bets for 3Q 2011 rate increase. Oct '11 prices in 40% chance for FOMC to raise funds rate to 0.5% at next Sept's meeting. That's down from 46% chance Tues and Wed, 62% chance Mon and 76% chance last Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2000 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD likely to have quieter session as market digests events of week, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Pair last 0.7247 versus 0.7244 late yesterday after subdued statement from RBNZ; "We expected an on-hold decision, and a more relaxed tone, but we were surprised by the extent that the RBNZ lowered its projections for the 90 day bill rate." Says topside moves, if attempted, likely limited to 0.7274 while support around 0.7200 should be enough to provide base. Notes strengthening EUR, AUD should help negate attempts to move lower at this point. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1957 GMT [Dow Jones] Canadian bonds end lower, taking direction from U.S. Treasurys in the absence of any significant domestic data or other market-moving events on the local front. The two-year bond is at 1.511% late vs 1.480% late Wed. The 10-year bond yields 2.976% vs 2.963%. There are no significant Canadian data releases Fri, but the calendar quickens next week, with Aug CPI data on Tues. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1955 GMT[Dow Jones] The yields are out on $2B Ukraine bonds. The $500M 5-year tranche will yield 6.875%, while the $1.5B 10-year tranche will yield 7.75%. Final pricing with coupon value is expected later Thu. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1950 GMT [Dow Jones] Near-dated Eurodollar futures prices settled a little higher as underlying 3-month dollar Libor was fixed lower. Modest losses for longer-dated Eurodollar contracts on spillover from hedge selling that jolted lengthy maturity Treasurys. Nearby Dec 2010 Eurodollars settled 0.5 BP higher, seeing 0.395% Libor at Dec. 13 contract expiration. That's down from 0.40% rate at Wed's settlement and 0.465% at last Wed's settlement. Longer-dated 2012-2013 contracts settled down as much as 4.5 BP. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1929 GMT [Dow Jones] As long-dated Treasurys bear the brunt of the selling for a second day and short end is little changed, their yield spreads widen. The benchmark curve, the yield spread between 2yr/10yr notes, widened to 228 bps, the steepest since Aug 10, up from 223 bps Wed. The gap dropped below 200 bps late last month but now the trend has reversed. Long-dated maturities, the market darlings in recent months until the end of August, have lost their harm s fear of double dip recession and deflation fades. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1926 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brigham Exploration\f28 \uc2 \f2  sold an upsized deal for $300M in 8.75% notes due 2018. The deal will enable the Austin, Texas-based oil and natural gas driller to refinance its 9 5/8% notes due 2014. Oversubscription for the debt allowed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 , to upsize it from the originally marketed $250M.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (katherine.greene@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1916 GMT [Dow Jones] Tsy Sec Geithner says Beijing's 1.5% appreciation of the yuan against the dollar in the last several months, "is too small to make a material difference" on the U.S. economy. Testifying before the House Ways and Means Committee that will soon consider legislation that penalizes Chinese imports, Geithner said he's targeting a much more substantial hike. Earlier, he said Beijing was moving "too slow," and pointed to China's previous appreciation of the yuan by 20% between 2005-2008 as a pace that would be more appropriate. (ian.talley@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 1610ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000dn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
