\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 10:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: READY TO DO MORE IN FACE OF ECONOMIC DOWNSIDE RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reiterated Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to implement further monetary policy steps, as the bank needs to pay more attention to downside risks to the domestic economy. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECONOMICS MINISTER BRUEDERLE: GERMAN 2010 GDP CLEARLY ABOVE +2.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economy could grow clearly above 2.5% this year, Germany's Economics Minister Rainer Bruederle said Thursday, adding that the recovery will deliver an upswing in employment. (published 0733 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA REGULATOR: NO NEW CAPITAL ADEQUACY REQUIREMENT FOR BIG BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator said Thursday it has no new capital adequacy requirement for major banks following media reports it is considering raising the capital requirement for the country's biggest lenders to as high as 15%. (published 0636 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ LEAVES SOME \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUNDS IN MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan pumped liquidity into the banking system Thursday, in a sign it is trying to help the government weaken the yen by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the money market. (published 0528 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN MOF OFFICIAL: WILL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WHEN APPROPRIATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market when deemed necessary, a finance ministry official said Thursday. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GEITHNER: 'VERY CONCERNED' ABOUT CHINA'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate has been "too slow," Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner will tell U.S. lawmakers Thursday, calling on a more balanced relationship between the two powers. (published 0022 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA YUAN HITS NEW HIGH AHEAD OF GEITHNER TESTIMONY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan hit another high against the U.S. dollar Thursday, despite the dollar's sharp rebound in international markets overnight, ahead of U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's testimony at a Congressional hearing on China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MAY REQUIRE 15% CAPITAL RATIO FOR BIGGEST LENDERS - BLOOMBERG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator may require the country's biggest lenders to boost their capital adequacy ratio to as high as 15% by the end of 2012, Bloomberg reported Thursday, citing a person familiar with the matter. (published 0403 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET MIN: MAY OUTDO 2010 DEFICIT AIM; SHOWS CRISIS EXIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Francois Baroin Thursday reiterated recent government comments that it could outperform its 2010 deficit target, saying this shows the country's economy to be emerging from the crisis. (published 0702 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBNZ HOLDS STEADY; MARKET PUSHES BACK HIKE FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand held interest rates steady Thursday and issued a surprisingly dovish policy statement, leading market players to push back their expectations for future rate hikes. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: SWISS GOVERNMENT LIFTS 2010 GDP GROWTH FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Switzerland's gross domestic product this year will likely grow faster than previously forecast, while consumer price inflation will be weaker than expected this year and next, the Swiss government said Thursday. (published 0545 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBA'S LOWE SAYS ECONOMY MORE EXPOSED THAN EVER TO CHINA SWINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is more exposed than ever to the vagaries of Chinese economic growth, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties, Phil Lowe, Assistant Governor at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA FINANCE MINISTER: ECONOMIC GROWTH WON'T BE LOWER THAN 8.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's economic growth won't be lower than 8.5% this fiscal year through March 2011, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Thursday. (published 0608 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA BUSINESS CONFIDENCE SLUMPS IN 3Q - WESTPAC-ACCI SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business confidence fell in the third quarter of 2010 compared with the previous quarter, a private sector survey showed Thursday. (published 0123 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA CONSUMER INFLATION EXPECTATIONS 3.1% IN SEPTEMBER VS 2.8% IN AUGUST-MI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian consumer inflationary expectations rose slightly in September after five straight months of declines, according to an industry survey published Thursday. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA EXPECTS KRW175 TLN TAX REVENUE 2010, KRW187.8 TLN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea raised its tax revenue forecast for this year as the domestic economy's recovery has boosted consumption and hence tax collection, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANK OF THAILAND\f28 \uc2 \f2 : STRONG BAHT A FACTOR AT NEXT RATE MEETING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday the strong baht will be a factor at the Monetary Policy Committee's next policy rate meeting in October, raising the prospect that it may hold rates steady. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAILAND KEEPS 2010 EXPORT GROWTH TARGET BUT FLAGS STRONG BAHT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's full-year export growth target remains intact at 20%, despite the baht's sharp rise against the U.S. dollar, Commerce Ministry Permanent Secretary Yanyong Phuangrach said Thursday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI AUGUST INDUSTRIAL SENTIMENT TURNS DOWN ON BAHT WORRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's industrial sentiment index turned lower in August, after rising for two straight months, as corporate executives became worried about the baht's strength, rising interest rates, and the likely economic slowdown in the second half. (published 0356 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY FLAT IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity was flat in August after a steady expansion in the first half of the year, data released Thursday showed. (published 2306 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA ECON PLANNER: CAN 'ABSOLUTELY' MEET 3% CPI TARGET THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic planning agency said late Wednesday the country can "absolutely" meet it target of around a 3% rise in the consumer price index this year, despite the CPI rising 3.5% from a year earlier in August. (published 0046 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TAIWAN REGULATOR: BANKS' CAPITAL POSITION STRENGTHENED IN PAST 2 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The capital position of Taiwan's domestic banks strengthened over the past two years and the lenders should be able to withstand a crisis similar to 2008's global financial crisis, the island's banking regulator said Wednesday. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA GOVERNMENT A$1.25B SEP 2030 INDEXED BONDS PRICE AT 2.66% REAL YIELD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian government said Thursday it has sold A$1.25 billion in 2.5% Sept. 20, 2030 Treasury-indexed bonds to the public. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BUND TO TEST 129.53 LOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Bear pressure is building on Monday's low at 129.53 following the setback off 130.79. The failure to hold onto support at 130.00 Wednesday suggests there is another bear wave to endure, threatening a break into fresh one-month lows towards a wave equality target at 129.09. Regaining ground above 130.41 is required to question the bear threat, opening 130.79. Weekly chart BUND trend: Bearish. (published 0512 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: GBP/USD HEADS HIGHER TO 1.5725 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Testing resistance at 1.3037 is still the main threat as dominant short-term bulls look to secure a foothold above 1.3000. A break above 1.3037 would open 1.3075, but Tuesday's break above 1.2920 has created scope for further gains towards the 1.3155/80 resistance cluster. Initial support lies at 1.2955, but corrective downside risk should be limited to the 1.2895 area. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Bullish. (published 0627 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: KIWI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHOULD EMBRACE THE CHINA EFFECT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand's forecasting pendulum has swung too far, too fast into gloomy territory. Demand from East Asia will ultimately steer New Zealand clear of its current malaise. (published 0647 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAY RAISE, NOT CUT, PRESSURE FOR YUAN ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the surface, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may appear to have temporarily frustrated international efforts to leverage faster \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reform in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: JAPANESE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WILL LIKELY HAVE A SEQUEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's "shock and awe" overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets has been effective, but it's almost certain to be repeated to ensure efforts to slow the yen's appreciation are successful. (published 1829 GMT) [ 16-09-10 0802GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
