\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Has Poor Track Record In Weakening Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 23:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--By one measure, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  outdid itself with Wednesday's one-trillion-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 3% decline in the yen was bigger than the one-day market reactions to any of seven comparably large yen-selling forays into the market this past decade.\par \par \uc2 But the real test will be whether the MOF succeeds in changing the yen's direction. And on that basis, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's track record is not very good. In each of those previous cases except one, the yen was stronger one month after the day of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in many cases considerably so.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the only time that the MOF successfully weakened the yen over a one-month period was in September 2001, when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  10 days after the Sept. 11 World Trade Center attacks. And in that case, the correction in the market might simply have been a reversal from the panicked positions put on after the attacks, which had driven the yen up 4% in a week.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the market's willingness to disregard the MOF was downright brazen. On Sept. 12, 2003, for example, Japanese authorities also sold one trillion yen for dollars. All that did on the day was to soften the yen a mere 0.2% against its U.S. counterpart. And a month later, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a hefty 7.5% stronger.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, this time could be different, and the sharp one-day impact may indicate that.\par \par \uc2 It seems clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught a lot of traders short dollars, in part because the re-election of Naoto Kan as Prime Minister over the weekend had been seen as a signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely. Also, in none of those prior cases since 2001 was the yen as strong as it was this time, having just recently reached a 15-year peak against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the long lag time between the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2004 and Wednesday's move marks a period in which the MOF has shown remarkable constraint compared with its past proclivities. After that prior \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed, the yen was traded freely and so gained 28% over the six-and-a-half-year period.\par \par \uc2 But earlier history from prior decades suggests that the only way the yen is likely to find some downward traction from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is when other countries jointly participate in it. And there is virtually no chance that the U.S. or Europe, both of which are worried about the strength of their own exporters, are likely to help out Japan in this effort. To do so would look hypocritical, given that both the U.S. and the E.U. have criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Yet what also seems likely given Japan's record this decade is that this won't be the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it undertakes on its own. Once it has been tempted into the market and failed to achieve its objectives, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a habit of coming back for another dip, as if taking a long time to get the message from the market that its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are futile.\par \par \uc2 The last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004 was the sixth in a year, most of them spread out within a couple of months of each other.\par \par \uc2 Dollar-yen traders might be in for a more interesting year.\par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2209; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 09-15-10 1747ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
